  Note: the app isn't wired to this yet — that's the next step. Here's the
  walkthrough.

  The chain, one link at a time

  The function run_dcf() answers one question: what is a share of NVIDIA worth 
  today, given a set of assumptions about the future? It gets there in five
  moves.

  1. Project revenue. You give it three growth rates, not a single one — a fast
  Year-1 rate, a slower Year-5 rate, and a modest long-run rate. The model
  glides between them in a straight line rather than jumping. So 50% → 41% → 32%
  → 24% → 15%, then drifting down to 3% by Year 10. This matters because a
  company growing 50% forever is worth infinity, which is not a useful answer.
  The taper is you saying "the hypergrowth fades."

  2. Turn revenue into cash. For each year, the same waterfall you'd build in
  Excel:

  ┌───────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┬─────────┐
  │           Step            │               Math               │ Year 1  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Revenue                   │ prior year × (1 + growth)        │ $195.8B │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ − Cost of goods           │ revenue × (1 − 75% gross margin) │ $48.9B  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ − Operating expenses      │ revenue × 25%                    │ $48.9B  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ = Operating income (EBIT) │                                  │ $97.9B  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ − Tax                     │ × 15%                            │ $14.7B  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ = NOPAT                   │ after-tax operating profit       │ $83.2B  │
  ├───────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ = Free cash flow          │ NOPAT × 90%                      │ $74.9B  │
  └───────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴─────────┘

  That last line is the simplifying assumption you asked for. In reality FCF is
  NOPAT plus depreciation minus capex minus working-capital changes. The 90%
  haircut stands in for all of it — it's a dial (fcf_conversion) you can turn
  later.

  3. Discount it. $1 in Year 5 isn't worth $1 today. Divide each year's cash
  flow by (1 + WACC) raised to the year number. At a 10% WACC, Year 10's $289.6B
  of cash is worth $111.7B today. Sum all ten years: $1,112B.

  4. Add the terminal value. The company doesn't evaporate in Year 10. The
  Gordon Growth formula — final-year FCF × (1 + terminal growth) ÷ (WACC −
  terminal growth) — prices every year after the forecast as a single lump sum:
  $4,261B in Year-10 dollars, or $1,643B discounted back to today.

  This is where the wacc <= terminal_growth guardrail earns its keep. If
  long-run growth ever meets or exceeds your discount rate, that denominator
  goes to zero or negative and the formula returns nonsense. The function raises
  an error instead of handing you a confident, wrong number.

  5. Bridge to per-share value. Enterprise value ($2,755B) is what the business
  is worth. Shareholders also own the cash and owe the debt, so: + $43.2B cash −
  $8.5B debt = $2,790B equity value ÷ 24.5B shares = $113.87 per share.

  Two things worth your attention

  Terminal value is 60% of the answer. Most of this valuation rests on a single
  formula about a year you can't see, not on the ten years you carefully
  projected. That's normal for a growth company and it's exactly why the WACC
  and terminal-growth sliders will swing the output hardest.

  At these assumptions NVIDIA looks overvalued — $113.87 against a $180 price,
  roughly 37% downside. Be skeptical of that number. I picked the operating
  assumptions (50% Y1 growth, 75% gross margin, 25% opex, 15% tax, 10% WACC) as
  reasonable-looking placeholders to make the function run, not from NVIDIA's
  filings. The starting balance-sheet figures are the ones you gave me; the
  drivers are mine and unvetted. Treat the $113.87 as proof the plumbing works,
  not as a view on the stock.

  Want me to wire these into the app as sliders next, so you can watch the
  per-share number move as you drag them?

