  What I built

  A 9 × 7 grid — terminal growth 1.0–5.0% down the rows, WACC 8–14% across the
  columns — where every cell revalues the company from scratch and prints its
  intrinsic value plus upside vs. the $180 price.

  One structural choice worth flagging: I moved the sliders outside the tabs.
  They now scope both views, so the heatmap always reflects your current growth,
  margin, tax and horizon settings — the grid isn't a static table, it's 63
  live valuations. Changing gross margin from 75% to 55% drops the grid's top
  value from $255 to $154.

  Why diverging color, not a single-hue ramp

  The question isn't "how big is this number" — it's "is this scenario above or 
  below what I'd pay." That's polarity, so the scale has a neutral gray midpoint
  pinned at fair value, blue for undervalued, red for overvalued. The break
  points are symmetric (±5/15/30/50%), and the ±15% band lines up with the
  BUY/SELL thresholds on the other tab — so the color boundary you care about is
  the same boundary the signal uses.

  I built the ramp by interpolating in OKLab from the neutral midpoint out to
  each pole and verified lightness moves monotonically outward in both light and
  dark mode. Each cell carries both themes as CSS variables, so it follows
  Gradio's theme rather than being baked to one. Every cell also prints its own
  number, so color never carries meaning alone — the heatmap is its own table
  view.

  What the grid shows

  value range across the grid: $61 .. $255
  value falls as WACC rises        (every row) ✓
  value rises with terminal growth (every column) ✓

  That directional check is a real sanity test on the math, and it holds
  everywhere. But look at the spread: the same company, same operating 
  assumptions, is worth $61 or $255 depending on two numbers that are 
  essentially judgment calls. At WACC 8% / terminal 5% it's a screaming buy; at
  14% / 1% it's worth a third of the market price. Almost none of that comes
  from the ten years of revenue you carefully modeled.

  For your purposes that's the real output of this tab — not any single cell,
  but the demonstration that a DCF's precision is mostly illusory. The honest
  use is to find which corner of this grid you can defend, not to read a price
  target off the middle.

  Caveat

  I verified the rendered HTML over HTTP — 63 cells, 9 distinct band colors, 9
  legend swatches, per-cell hover tooltips, both themes emitted. But I still 
  can't see the page, so column widths, wrapping, and how the colors actually
  feel on screen are unconfirmed. Tell me if the dark-mode cells look muddy —
  the neutral end of a diverging ramp on a dark surface is the spot most likely
  to need a nudge.

