● Answer: most of the gap was my bad assumption, not the market's optimism.
  Here's the decomposition.

  1. First, a correction to my own model

  I flagged earlier that the operating assumptions were placeholders. One of
  them was materially wrong. My defaults — 75% gross margin minus 25% opex —
  implied a 50% operating margin. NVIDIA's actual FY2025 operating margin was
  62.4%. I had opex at roughly double reality (~12.6% of revenue).

  Fixing that alone, plus NVIDIA's ~13% effective tax rate:

  ┌───────────────────────────────┬─────────────┬──────────┐
  │                               │ Value/share │ vs. $180 │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────┼──────────┤
  │ My original placeholders      │ $113.87     │ −36.7%   │
  ├───────────────────────────────┼─────────────┼──────────┤
  │ Real operating margin (62.4%) │ $145.05     │ −19.4%   │
  └───────────────────────────────┴─────────────┴──────────┘

  So $31 of the original $66 gap was my error. The remaining $35 is genuine
  disagreement with the market.

  Useful wrinkle: the model only ever uses gross margin minus opex, so if I
  split the 62.4% wrongly between the two, it doesn't change the answer. Only
  the operating margin needs to be right.

  2. What it's actually sensitive to

  Effect of one percentage point on each assumption:

  ┌──────────────────────────┬───────────────┬────────┐
  │        Assumption        │ Δ value/share │  Δ %   │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ WACC                     │ −$15.78       │ −13.9% │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Terminal growth          │ +$14.51       │ +12.7% │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Forecast horizon (+1 yr) │ +$4.51        │ +4.0%  │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Year-5 growth            │ +$3.68        │ +3.2%  │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Gross margin             │ +$2.25        │ +2.0%  │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Opex %                   │ −$2.25        │ −2.0%  │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Year-1 growth            │ +$1.95        │ +1.7%  │
  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────┼────────┤
  │ Tax rate                 │ −$1.32        │ −1.2%  │
  └──────────────────────────┴───────────────┴────────┘

  WACC and terminal growth are ~8x more powerful than next year's revenue 
  growth. This is the uncomfortable truth of DCF: the two inputs that dominate
  the answer are the two nobody can observe. Year-1 growth — the number analysts
  fight over every quarter, the thing the earnings call is about — barely moves
  it.

  That's the same fact the heatmap tab shows geometrically. These two are on
  those axes for a reason.

  3. What the market is implicitly assuming

  Reverse-DCF from the corrected base — each row is what that assumption alone
  would have to be to justify $180, holding everything else fixed:

  ┌─────────────────┬───────┬────────────────────────────┐
  │   Assumption    │ Base  │       Market implies       │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ Year-1 growth   │ 50%   │ 63%                        │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ Year-5 growth   │ 15%   │ 21.8%                      │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ Terminal growth │ 3%    │ 4.7%                       │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ WACC            │ 10%   │ 8.8%                       │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ Gross margin    │ 75%   │ 90.2% (implausible)        │
  ├─────────────────┼───────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ Opex %          │ 12.6% │ unreachable — even at zero │
  └─────────────────┴───────┴────────────────────────────┘

  That last row is the sharpest finding. Even if NVIDIA spent nothing on R&D and
  SG&A forever, cost control cannot justify $180. The market's optimism is
  structurally not about margins — it has to be about growth or the discount
  rate.

  Pick the growth story and it means: $978B of revenue in year 10, throwing off
  $478B of annual free cash flow. For scale, the entire global semiconductor 
  industry did about $792B in 2025. The market is pricing NVIDIA alone to
  out-bill the whole industry's current size within a decade.

  Pick the discount-rate story instead and it's milder: an 8.8% WACC rather than
  10% — i.e. investors see NVIDIA as meaningfully safer than my assumption,
  which for a company with this customer concentration is a real argument, just
  not an obviously winning one.

  The honest read

  At $180 the market is not making a margin bet or a next-year bet. It is making
  a terminal bet — that AI demand compounds long enough and NVIDIA's moat holds
  long enough that either the growth runway or the risk profile looks
  materially better than a generic 10%/3% frame allows. My corrected model says
  that's roughly a 20% stretch, which is well inside the range where reasonable
  people disagree, and well inside my own assumptions' error bars.

  Two caveats on my numbers: the 62.4% operating margin is from NVIDIA's FY2025
  results, and the 90% FCF conversion is still a placeholder (actual FY2025 was
  closer to 86%, which would shade value down a few percent).

  Want me to change the app's opex default from 25% to 12.6% so the sliders open
  on a realistic NVIDIA rather than a strawman? That's a one-line change and
  it's the honest starting point.

  Sources: NVIDIA FY2025 results · NVIDIA operating margin · SIA 2025 
  semiconductor sales · Gartner 2025 semiconductor revenue

