     Rework the DCF input controls

     Context

     The model's input set is too coarse to express a real forecast. Three
     specific gaps:

     1. Growth is locked to a taper. You can only set Year 1 and Year 5 and let
        the model
        glide between them. There is no way to say "Year 3 is the bad year."
     2. Margin is a single constant applied to every year, so margin compression
        — the
        central question for a company earning 62% operating margins — cannot be
        modelled.
     3. Free cash flow is a fudge. fcf_conversion=0.90 in dcf.py:121 stands in
        for
        capex and working capital rather than modelling either. It is the
        crudest assumption
        in the file.

     There is also a redundancy to clear out. In dcf.py:117-119, EBIT is
     revenue × gross_margin − revenue × opex_pct, so those two inputs only ever
     act through
     their difference. An explicit operating-margin control makes both obsolete
     — which
     settles your step 4: they are not needed, and they go.

     Outcome: growth and margin become per-year assumptions behind a mode
     toggle, and free
     cash flow gets built from real drivers instead of a 90% haircut.

     Decisions made

     - Single net-capex slider (capex net of depreciation) rather than separate
       capex and
       D&A dials. FCF = NOPAT − NetCapex − ΔNWC.
     - One Taper/Per-year toggle governs both growth and operating margin, so
       the two
       stay at matching detail. Taper shows 4 sliders; Per-year swaps in 10.
     - Gross margin and opex % are deleted — sliders, model parameters, and the
       gross_profit / opex fields on YearRow.
     - fcf_conversion is deleted, superseded by the explicit drivers.

     Model changes — dcf.py

     Schedules

     Replace growth_schedule with two functions sharing one taper helper:

     - growth_schedule(year_1, year_5, terminal, horizon, explicit=None) — years
       1-5 come
       from the taper or verbatim from explicit; years 6..N keep today's
       behaviour, tapering
       from the Year-5 rate to terminal growth.
     - margin_schedule(year_1, year_5, horizon, explicit=None) — same for years
       1-5;
       years 6..N hold flat at the Year-5 margin. (Margins have no natural
       "terminal"
       analogue. Flat is the neutral choice; easy to change to a taper later.)

     Per-year cash flow build

     revenue_t     = revenue_{t-1} × (1 + growth_t)
     EBIT_t        = revenue_t × operating_margin_t
     NOPAT_t       = EBIT_t × (1 − tax_rate)
     net_capex_t   = revenue_t × net_capex_pct
     ΔNWC_t        = (revenue_t − revenue_{t-1}) × nwc_pct_of_growth
     FCF_t         = NOPAT_t − net_capex_t − ΔNWC_t

     Working capital keys off revenue growth, not revenue level — this is the
     standard
     treatment and the only one that behaves in perpetuity. Tying it to the
     level would make
     working capital consume a fixed share of revenue forever; tying it to the
     delta means it
     shrinks toward nothing as growth approaches the terminal rate, which is
     what the Gordon
     Growth formula assumes. Flat revenue correctly produces ΔNWC = 0.

     YearRow gains operating_margin, net_capex, change_in_nwc; loses
     gross_profit,
     opex. Terminal value, the cash/debt bridge, and sensitivity_grid are
     untouched.

     UI changes — app.py

     Slider panel, reorganised into four labelled sections (existing
     gr.Markdown("### ...")
     pattern):

     ┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │   Section    │                         Controls                         │
     ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Forecast     │ gr.Radio — Taper | Per-year                              │
     │ detail       │                                                          │
     ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Growth &     │ Taper: Y1/Y5 growth, Y1/Y5 margin (4)<br>Per-year:       │
     │ margin       │ growth Y1-Y5, margin Y1-Y5 (10)                          │
     ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Cash flow    │ Net capex (% of revenue), Working capital (% of revenue  │
     │              │ growth), Tax rate                                        │
     ├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Discount     │ WACC, Terminal growth, Forecast years                    │
     │ rate         │                                                          │
     └──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     The two slider sets live in gr.Group() containers; the radio's .change
     flips their
     visible flag via gr.update. Hidden components still hold values, so every
     slider
     stays in the inputs list and the mode value selects which set the function
     reads.

     That pushes valuate() to ~20 inputs. Pass inputs as a set so Gradio hands
     the
     function a single component-keyed dict rather than 20 positional args — 20
     positional
     parameters is a real bug risk on any future reorder. If Gradio 6 misbehaves
     with the set
     form, fall back to an explicit ordered list.

     Small addition: a Year-1 build table above the existing bridge — revenue →
     EBIT →
     NOPAT → − net capex → − ΔWC → FCF — reusing the existing .bridge CSS class.
     Without it
     the two new sliders have no visible effect except on the final number. Say
