  What changed

  1. Taper / per-year toggle — a radio at the top of the panel swaps between 4
  sliders (Y1→Y5 growth and margin) and 10 (each year set individually).
  Verified in /config: the radio's change event drives the two groups'
  visibility, and they open in the right state.

  2. Operating margin by year — replaces gross margin and opex. Beyond Year 5
  the margin holds flat at the Y5 level; growth still tapers to terminal as
  before.

  3. Cash flow is now built, not fudged. fcf_conversion = 0.90 is gone:

  FCF = NOPAT − net capex − ΔWorking capital

  Working capital keys off revenue growth, not revenue level. That matters more
  than it sounds: tied to the level, working capital would eat a fixed share of
  revenue forever and quietly corrupt the terminal value. Tied to the delta, it
  fades as growth approaches the terminal rate — which is what Gordon Growth
  assumes. Flat revenue correctly consumes zero.

  4. Gross margin and opex removed. Your step 4, confirmed: EBIT only ever used
  their difference, so they were redundant the moment margin became explicit.

  Verification

  The property I most wanted to prove — the toggle is an input style, not a
  second model:

  baseline (taper)        $  133.70   -25.7%  SELL
  per-year, same schedule $  133.70   mode switch is a no-op: True
  per-year, Y3 growth 0%  $  103.40   (per-year editing bites)
  per-year, Y4 margin 30% $  130.82   (margin-by-year bites)

    net capex 1.5 -> 10%   $  108.72  ( -24.98)
    working cap 10 -> 40%  $  127.14  (  -6.56)
    Y5 margin 55 -> 40%    $   99.16  ( -34.54)
    WACC 10 -> 8%          $  193.52  ( +59.82)

  Heatmap still renders 63 cells across 9 bands; the WACC guardrail still
  returns its warning card.

  Two things I changed my mind on mid-build

  The set-input trick backfired. The plan called for passing inputs as a set so
  the function got a keyed dict instead of 21 positional args. It worked, but it
  destroyed the named API — parameters came back as param_1..param_20 ordered
  by set iteration, which isn't stable across restarts. I reverted to an ordered
  list with explicit names and instead wrote test_app_wiring.py to guard the
  risk I was originally avoiding: it asserts the component list and the function
  signature stay in lockstep, by label and position. That catches a silent
  misassignment, which is the failure mode that would otherwise produce a
  plausible wrong number.

  The opening value is $133.70, not the ~$145 I projected. My plan estimate
  didn't account for the new drivers. Decomposed:

