● The Projection tab is live at http://127.0.0.1:7860. 49 tests pass (was 34).

  The chart

  Three lines on one shared axis — revenue, free cash flow, and PV of free cash
  flow, all in $B. Same units, so no second scale is needed and the dual-axis
  trap doesn't arise.

  The form wasn't a taste call: the data's job is trend over time with distinct 
  series, which the heuristic resolves to multi-line with categorical colour.
  Built as inline SVG rather than a plotting library, so it themes through the
  same CSS-variable mechanism as the heatmap and adds no dependency.

  The hover is a pure-CSS crosshair — one transparent band per year, revealing a
  hairline, a dot on each line, and a tooltip listing all three values. No
  JavaScript, deliberately: Gradio may not execute script injected through
  gr.HTML, and a silently dead hover layer is worse than none. Tooltips flip to
  the left past the midpoint so they never run off the edge, decided at render
  time since there's no JS to measure.

  Palette — validated, not eyeballed

  light  CVD ΔE 9.2  normal ΔE 27.6   WARN aqua 2.74:1 contrast
  dark   CVD ΔE 9.4  normal ΔE 26.5   all PASS

  That light-mode WARN obligates relief, which is why every line carries a 
  direct endpoint label. Those labels are an accessibility requirement here, not
  decoration — which matters for what happened next.

  The bug the sweep caught

  The method's final step is "render it and look at it," and I have no browser.
  So I swept 500 slider combinations checking label geometry numerically instead
  — and found that at a short horizon with low WACC and thin margins, the three
  endpoint labels collapsed to 0.7px apart. Unreadable, and it would have
  silently broken the contrast relief.

  My first fix was wrong in an instructive way: it shifted the whole label group
  to escape the bottom edge, which dragged an unrelated label off the top and
  collapsed two others to 0.0px apart. The sweep caught that too. The working
  fix is a forward-then-backward pass that only moves labels that actually
  collide. Worst separation across all 500 scenarios is now exactly 13.0px, with
  nothing off-canvas — and that sweep is now a test.

  Verification

  - Live chart geometry inverts back to the engine: worst error $0.14B, which is
    sub-pixel coordinate rounding on an $800B axis
  - Hover bands track the horizon (10 → 20); narrowest hit target 30px at
    maximum horizon, against a 24px minimum
  - Guardrail warns in the chart panel with no half-drawn SVG
  - Valuation and Sensitivity untouched — table still 10 rows, heatmap still 63
    cells

  Outstanding: I verified colour and geometry numerically, but nobody has
  actually looked at this chart. Line weights, label placement and how the
  crosshair feels in practice are unconfirmed. Worth a glance when you open it.

  Two notes

  - The tab carries a one-line explanation of why the PV line matters: FCF
    climbs every year, but its present value peaks at year 7 and then falls —
    discounting outrunning growth. That's the mechanism behind terminal value
    being 60% of the answer, and it was invisible in the table.
  - README.md needs the new tab documented. Nothing is committed yet — viz.py,
    app.py, test_app_wiring.py modified, test_viz.py new. Say the word and I'll
    update the README and commit.

