  ⎿  Current Plan

     Add a Projection tab with a forecast chart

     Context

     The forecast is currently readable only as a 10-row table of numbers. Shape
     is the one
     thing a table cannot show: whether growth is decelerating, how far free
     cash flow trails
     revenue, and — the point worth seeing — that the present value of each
     year's cash flow
     peaks and then falls even while the cash flow itself keeps climbing.

     That last curve is why terminal value dominates the valuation, and right
     now nothing in
     the app makes it visible. A chart does.

     Form — chosen by the data's job, not by taste

     The data is three measures over time. The form heuristic gives: trend over 
     time →
     line; distinct series → multi-line, categorical colour.

     Critically, all three series are $B on one shared axis. Revenue peaks at
     $757B and
     PV of FCF at $147B, which is a wide but workable range — and because the
     units match,
     the dual-axis trap (the single most common charting error) does not arise.
     One axis,
     three lines.

     ┌─────────────┬───────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┐
     │   Series    │               Shape               │     Palette slot      │
     ├─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
     │ Revenue     │ $195.8B → $757.1B, decelerating   │ 1 — blue #2a78d6 /    │
     │             │                                   │ #3987e5 dark          │
     ├─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
     │ Free cash   │ $94.4B → $340.4B, monotonic       │ 2 — orange #eb6834 /  │
     │ flow        │                                   │ #d95926 dark          │
     ├─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┤
     │ PV of FCF   │ rises to $147.2B at year 7, then  │ 3 — aqua #1baf7a /    │
     │             │ falls to $131.2B                  │ #199e70 dark          │
     └─────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┘

     Palette — validated, not eyeballed

     Ran validate_palette.js on both themes before designing anything:

     - Two-series case: all checks PASS, CVD ΔE 24.7 light / 26.8 dark.
     - Three-series case (what we're shipping): all checks PASS in both themes,
       worst
       all-pairs CVD ΔE 9.2 light / 9.4 dark — clear of the ≥8 target.
     - One WARN: light-mode aqua sits at 2.74:1 against the surface, below 3:1.
       The
       documented palette is not re-stepped; instead the relief rule applies and
       is
       non-negotiable: every line carries a visible direct endpoint label. That
       turns an
       optional nicety into a requirement, so it is specified below rather than
       left to taste.

     Chart build — new render_projection_chart(result) in viz.py

     Inline SVG, no new dependency. The app is hand-built HTML/CSS with an OKLab
     colour
     system already in viz.py; a plotting library would look like a different
     product and
     would not respect the palette. SVG also themes the same way the heatmap
     does — both
     light and dark values emitted per element as CSS custom properties,
     switched by the
     existing .dark / prefers-color-scheme rules.

     Marks, to the spec sheet:

     - Lines 2px, round join and cap.
     - End markers r=4 (8px) filled with the series colour, carrying a 2px ring 
       in the
       surface colour so they stay legible where lines cross.
     - Gridlines and axes 1px solid hairline, recessive — never dashed.
     - Legend always present (3 series), keyed with short line strokes rather
       than filled
       boxes.
     - Direct labels on all three endpoints (see the WARN above). No number on
       any other
       point — the axis, legend and hover carry the rest.
     - All text in text tokens, never the series colour.
     - Y scale runs 0 → max revenue snapped to a nice round step; X ticks are
       year numbers.

     Hover — a pure-CSS crosshair

     The spec for a line chart is a crosshair that finds the X and one tooltip
     listing every
     series at that X. Doing that in JavaScript is risky here: Gradio may not
     execute script
     injected through gr.HTML, and a silently dead hover layer is worse than
     none.

     So build it in CSS. One transparent full-height <rect> per forecast year;
     :hover on
     that band reveals a vertical hairline, a dot on each of the three lines,
     and a tooltip
     listing the year and all three values. No JavaScript at all.

     - Band width is plot width ÷ years — 62px at a 10-year horizon, 31px at 20
       — so the
       ≥24px hit-target minimum holds even at maximum horizon.
     - Edge flipping without JS: the renderer knows each band's index, so bands
       past the
       midpoint get their tooltip emitted to the left of the hairline at build
       time.
     - Values lead, series names follow; tooltip rows keyed with a line stroke.

     The chart's table-view twin is the forecast table, which stays in the
     Valuation tab.

     Wiring — app.py

     - New gr.Tab("Projection") between Valuation and Sensitivity, holding the
       chart plus a
       one-line note on what the PV line shows.
     - build_valuation() returns three panels now (valuation, chart, heatmap);
       valuate
       and the gr.on outputs list grow to match.
     - The WACC ≤ terminal guardrail must return the warning card for the chart
       panel too —
       never a half-drawn SVG.
     - New .chart CSS block beside the existing .proj / .hm rules.

     Tests

     test_viz.py (new) — the chart must report the engine, the same discipline
     the table got:

     - Plotted points invert back to the model. Parse each polyline's
       coordinates, invert
       the y-scale, and confirm they recover revenue, free_cash_flow and
       pv_of_fcf to
       within rounding. A chart drawing a shape the engine never produced is the
       failure that
       matters, and it is invisible to "does it render".
     - Point count per series tracks the horizon (10 → 20), as does the
       hover-band count.
     - Endpoint direct labels match the model's final-year values.
     - Series colours are exactly the documented slots, both themes — guards
       against a
       later "tidy-up" quietly breaking a validated palette.

     test_app_wiring.py — valuate returns three outputs; the guardrail yields a
     warning
     with no <svg> in the chart panel.

     Verification

