     /plan to edit
     Add a Valuation Waterfall tab

     Context

     The app can now show what the forecast is (the Projection chart) and what
     the
     valuation totals (the equity bridge table), but not how the second is built
     out of the
     first. The bridge exists only as a column of numbers, so the single most
     important fact
     about this valuation — that the terminal value contributes more than every
     projected year
     combined — is something you have to work out by comparing two figures
     rather than see.

     A waterfall makes the composition structural: each bar is a contribution,
     and the running
     total carries you from discounted cash flows to equity value.

     Form and colour

     The skill's form table has no waterfall entry, so this is the method
     applied rather than
     a rule quoted. The data's job is contribution to a running total, which is
     what a
     waterfall is for — and it is what you asked for.

     The colour job is polarity: does this component add value or remove it.
     That is the
     diverging case, and viz.py already owns a validated diverging pair from the
     heatmap.
     Reusing it keeps the whole app on one semantic: blue adds value, red 
     subtracts — the
     same meaning those hues already carry in the sensitivity grid.

     ┌──────────────────┬─────────┬─────────┬─────────────────────────┐
     │       Role       │  Light  │  Dark   │ Contrast (light / dark) │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────────────────────┤
     │ Increase         │ #2a78d6 │ #3987e5 │ 4.42 / 5.26             │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────────────────────┤
     │ Decrease         │ #e34948 │ #e66767 │ 3.95 / 5.93             │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────┼─────────┼─────────────────────────┤
     │ Subtotal & total │ #898781 │ #898781 │ 3.59 / 5.33             │
     └──────────────────┴─────────┴─────────┴─────────────────────────┘

     All three clear 3:1 against the app's real surfaces. The neutral is the
     palette's muted
     token and is mode-invariant. The categorical validator is deliberately not 
     run on this
     set — it fails diverging ramps by design; the relevant checks are the
     documented pair
     plus per-fill contrast, done above.

     Crucially, colour is not the only channel: direction of travel (bar rises
     or falls)
     and a signed value label on every bar both carry the same information, so a
     reader who
     cannot separate blue from red loses nothing.

     The bars

     Vertical columns, the finance convention:

     ┌─────┬─────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┬──────────────┐
     │  #  │           Bar           │           Type           │   Default    │
     │     │                         │                          │    value     │
     ├─────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┤
     │ 1   │ PV of forecast cash     │ increase from zero       │ $1,310.0B    │
     │     │ flows                   │                          │              │
     ├─────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┤
     │ 2   │ PV of terminal value    │ increase                 │ $1,931.0B    │
     ├─────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┤
     │ 3   │ Enterprise value        │ subtotal, anchored at    │ $3,241.1B    │
     │     │                         │ zero                     │              │
     ├─────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┤
     │ 4   │ Net cash / Net debt     │ increase or decrease     │ +$34.7B      │
     ├─────┼─────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────┤
     │ 5   │ Equity value            │ total, anchored at zero  │ $3,275.8B    │
     └─────┴─────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────┘

     Two behaviours that must be built in rather than assumed:

     - The label flips with the sign. NVIDIA holds more cash than debt, so
       net_debt is
       −34.7 and the bar adds value. A bar labelled "Net debt" that goes up is a
       lie; it
       reads "Net cash" whenever net_debt < 0, matching the bridge table's
       existing
       wording.
     - Components can go negative. At thin margins with heavy capex both PV
       components
       invert (pv_of_forecast −573, pv_of_terminal −799, equity −1,337). The
       y-scale must span
       a negative domain and bars must be able to sit below the zero line.

     A limitation to state rather than design around: net debt is 1% of equity
     value, so
     that bar will be a sliver at any honest shared scale. That is true
     information — net debt
     barely matters for this company — so the value label carries it and the
     scale is left
     alone. No broken axis, no second scale.

     Chart build — render_waterfall(result) in viz.py

     Inline SVG beside render_projection_chart, reusing its geometry helpers
     (_nice_ceiling
     for the axis, _decollide if labels crowd) and the existing theming
     approach: light and
     dark values emitted per element as CSS custom properties, switched by the
     .dark /
     prefers-color-scheme rules already in app.py.

     - Columns capped at 24 user units per the mark spec, clustered in a centred
       plot
       region so the cap reads as deliberate air rather than scattered bars.
     - 4px rounded data-end, square where it meets its baseline.
     - Connector hairlines from each bar's end to the next bar's start — 1px
       solid,
       recessive, the same treatment as gridlines.
     - A zero line whenever the domain includes negatives.
     - Value label on every bar, signed (+1,931, −8.5). Five labelled bars is
       the
       waterfall norm, not the "number on every point" the spec warns about —
       that rule is
       about flooding a dense series.
     - Category labels wrapped onto two lines below the axis (PV of forecast /
       cash flows), so nothing is angled or clipped at ~124 units per band.
     - Caption under the chart does the division the chart cannot: ÷ 24.5B 
       shares = $133.70
       per share, vs $180.00 market price.

     Hover: bars use per-mark tooltips, not a crosshair — on a bar chart the
     mark is the
     hit target. Pure CSS again, for the same reason as the projection chart
     (Gradio may not
     execute injected script). Each band carries a transparent hit rect wider
     than its 24-unit
     bar, clearing the 24px minimum.

     Wiring — app.py

     - New gr.Tab("Valuation Waterfall") after Projection, before the
       sensitivity tab.
     - build_valuation() returns four panels; valuate, its signature and the
       gr.on
       outputs list grow to match. test_app_wiring.py already asserts the count,
       so it fails
       loudly if one is missed.
     - The WACC ≤ terminal guardrail returns the warning card for the waterfall
       panel too.
     - New .wf CSS block beside .chart / .proj / .hm.

     Tests — extend test_viz.py

     Same discipline as the projection chart: the picture must report the
     engine.

     - Bar geometry inverts back to the model. Parse each rect's y/height,
       invert the
       scale, and recover pv_of_forecast, pv_of_terminal, enterprise_value,
       net_debt
       and equity_value.
     - The running total actually chains — each floating bar starts where the
       previous one
       ended, and the two anchored bars start at zero. This is the specific way
       a waterfall
       goes wrong while still looking fine.
     - Negative components render below the zero line and the axis domain
       includes them
       (using the thin-margin/heavy-capex scenario above).
     - The net-debt bar flips label and colour with its sign — "Net cash" and
       blue when
       the company holds net cash, "Net debt" and red when it does not.
     - Fills are exactly the documented diverging poles and the neutral.
     - Hit targets ≥24px; a sweep over slider combinations asserting nothing
       renders
       off-canvas.

     Verification

     1. .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q — full suite green (49 existing +
        new).
     2. Restart; confirm HTTP 200 and four tabs in /config, with four outputs on
        valuate.
     3. Drive the live server with gradio_client: invert the rendered bar
        geometry and
        reconcile against run_dcf(); confirm the chain; force a
        net-debt-positive case by
        pushing cash below debt and confirm the bar flips; confirm the guardrail
        warns with no
        <svg>.
     4. Confirm the other three tabs are untouched — table reconciles,
        projection chart
        reconciles, heatmap still 63 cells / 9 bands.

     Still cannot complete the method's final step. "Render it and look at it"
     needs eyes,
     and browser tools are not connected. I will substitute numeric geometry
     checks — bars and
     labels in range, no collisions, connectors meeting the bars they join — and
     say plainly
     that visual confirmation is outstanding. The projection chart's 0.7px label
     collision was
     caught exactly this way, so the substitute has earned some trust, but it is
     not the same
     thing as looking.

     Out of scope

     - No change to the valuation math, the forecast table, the equity bridge,
