  Five vertical columns carrying the bridge from discounted cash flows to equity
  value:

  bar                          model hi   drawn hi   model lo   drawn lo
  PV of forecast cash flows      1310.0     1309.9        0.0        0.0
  PV of terminal value           3241.1     3240.8     1310.0     1309.9
  Enterprise value               3241.1     3240.8        0.0        0.0
  Net cash                       3275.8     3276.1     3241.1     3240.8
  Equity value                   3275.8     3276.1        0.0        0.0

  Worst reconciliation error against the engine: $0.29B on a $3,400B axis —
  coordinate rounding. The chain is exact: 1,310 + 1,931 = 3,241 EV, + 34.7 =
  3,276 equity.

  Colour reuses the heatmap's validated diverging pair, so the app now speaks
  one language: blue adds value, red subtracts, grey is a running total — the
  same meaning those hues already carry in the sensitivity grid. All three clear
  3:1 on both surfaces (4.42 / 3.95 / 3.59 light). Direction of travel and a
  signed value label carry the same information, so colour is never the only
  channel.

  Two things built rather than assumed

  The net-debt bar's label follows its sign. NVIDIA holds net cash, so that bar
  adds value — and a bar reading "Net debt" that pushes the total up would be a
  lie. It reads "Net cash" in blue at defaults; forcing cash below debt flips it
  to "Net debt" in red. Both verified.

  Components can go negative. At 2% margins with 20% capex both present values
  invert. The scale spans a negative domain, a zero line appears, and bars
  extend below it — tested against that scenario rather than assumed away.

  A limitation I left alone

  Net debt is $34.7B against $3,276B equity — 1% of the total, so that bar is a
  sliver. I didn't break the axis or add a second scale to flatter it; the value
  label carries it. That thinness is true information: net debt barely matters
  for this company. The terminal value bar reaching further than every projected
  year combined is the same chart telling you where the valuation actually
  comes from.

