     Add a second sensitivity heatmap: operating margin vs. terminal growth

     Context

     The existing sensitivity grid varies WACC against terminal growth — the two
     inputs
     nobody can observe. But the margin assumption is doing comparable work and
     has no grid of
     its own: tapering the Year-5 margin from 62.4% to 55% is worth about
     $17/share, more than
     net capex and working capital combined. A second heatmap on the same
     terminal-growth axis
     lets those two be read side by side.

     The form question is already settled by the existing tab: a grid of one
     value across two
     varied inputs is a heatmap, and this one reuses the same validated
     diverging scale and the
     same band semantics. Consistency here is the feature — the second grid
     should be readable
     by anyone who has read the first.

     What the margin axis varies

     The Year-5 operating margin, which holds flat from Year 5 into perpetuity.
     One
     variable, and the axis label says exactly what it is. It also pairs
     coherently with
     terminal growth: both govern the end state, so the grid reads as "what is
     the terminal
     economy worth".

     Axis: 40% → 70% in 5pt steps (7 columns), bracketing both the 55% default
     and
     NVIDIA's actual 62.4%. Rows stay the existing terminal growth 1–5% in 0.5pt
     steps (9
     rows), so the grid is the same 9×7 shape as the WACC one.

     Two traps found while investigating

     1. year_5_margin is silently ignored in per-year mode. When the sliders are
     set to
     "Set each year", shared carries operating_margins=[...], and run_dcf then
     takes the
     list and ignores year_5_margin entirely. A naive grid would render seven
     identical
     columns — a heatmap that looks fine and varies nothing. Verified: passing
     both
     operating_margins and year_5_margin=20% returns the unchanged $133.70.

     The fix is a documented helper in dcf.py rather than a special case buried
     in the UI:

     def apply_year_5_margin(assumptions, margin):
         """Set the Year-5 margin whichever way the caller expressed margins."""
         # In per-year mode the explicit list wins over year_5_margin, so the 
     axis has
         # to reach into the list or it silently does nothing.

     2. The None path stops being dead. The WACC grid never produces empty
     cells, because
     every WACC on its axis (8–14%) exceeds every terminal growth (1–5%). The
     margin grid holds
     WACC at the slider value, so setting WACC to 4% makes every row at or above
     4% terminal
     growth invalid. render_heatmap already draws those as "–"; that branch just
     goes from
     theoretical to reachable, and now needs a test.

     Changes

     dcf.py

     Generalise sensitivity_grid to take axis names rather than hardcoding WACC
     and
     terminal growth:

     def sensitivity_grid(x_param, x_values, y_param, y_values, **assumptions)

     Rows are y_values, columns are x_values, matching the current layout. Axis
     values are
     applied through a small _apply_axis() that routes year_5_margin through the
     helper
     above and everything else to a plain keyword. The existing call becomes
     sensitivity_grid("wacc", WACC_AXIS, "terminal_growth", TERMINAL_AXIS, 
     **shared) and must
     produce a byte-identical grid — asserted by test.

     viz.py

     render_heatmap gains x_label / y_label (defaulting to the current WACC
     wording) so
     the corner cell and the per-cell tooltip name the right axes. No change to
     the colour
     scale, the bands, the legend or the markup — the second grid is the same
     component with
     different inputs.

     app.py

     - New gr.Tab("Sensitivity - Operating Margin vs. Terminal Growth")
       immediately after the
       existing sensitivity tab, keeping the two grids adjacent.
     - MARGIN_AXIS = [0.40, 0.45, 0.50, 0.55, 0.60, 0.65, 0.70].
     - build_valuation() returns five panels; valuate, its signature and the
       gr.on
       outputs grow to match. The panel-count guard in test_app_wiring.py will
       fail loudly if
       one is missed — it already caught this on the last tab.
     - Guardrail returns the warning card for the new panel too.
     - No new CSS: the grid reuses .hm.

     Tests

     - The generalised grid reproduces the old one exactly — the WACC tab must
       not shift.
     - The margin axis bites in per-year mode. Build a margin grid with
       operating_margins set and assert the columns differ. This is the trap
       above, and it is
       invisible to "does it render".
     - Direction: value rises across the margin axis and rises down the
       terminal-growth
       axis, at every row and column.
     - Empty cells appear when WACC sits below part of the terminal-growth axis,
       and render
       as "–" rather than raising.
     - render_heatmap labels the axes it is given; the two tabs get different
       corner text.
     - Wiring: five panels, all five warn on the guardrail.

