● Everything verified. The fifth tab is live at http://127.0.0.1:7860. 64 tests 
  pass (was 57).

  The grid

  63 cells, same 9×7 shape and same diverging scale as the WACC grid, sharing
  its terminal-growth rows so the two read against each other. Corner labels
  confirm the axes are named correctly:

  WACC grid  : Terminal growth ↓ / WACC →
  margin grid: Terminal growth ↓ / Year 5 operating margin →

  All four corners reconcile against direct run_dcf() calls, and value rises
  monotonically across margin and down terminal growth.

  Three bugs caught during the build

  1. The margin axis was inert in per-year mode. run_dcf lets an explicit
  operating_margins list win over year_5_margin, so setting the keyword alone
  does nothing — the grid would have rendered seven identical columns and looked
  entirely convincing. Verified live: per-year mode now varies across columns
  and is identical to taper mode.

  2. The same axis meant two different things in the two modes. In taper mode,
  Y5 margin is the taper's endpoint, so moving it dragged years 2–4 along;
  per-year mode moved only year 5. The same question — "what if the persistent
  margin were 40%?" — got two answers depending on an input toggle.
  apply_year_5_margin now materializes the first five margins and replaces only
  the fifth, so both modes agree. This one surfaced because the mode-equivalence
  test failed; it would otherwise have shipped silently.

  3. The margin grid ignored your WACC slider. shared deliberately omits wacc
  because the other grid varies it as an axis. This grid doesn't, so every cell
  was quietly using run_dcf's 10% default. Now verified: dropping WACC to 8%
  moves the corner from $85 to $112.

  Also, the None path in render_heatmap stopped being theoretical — this grid
  holds WACC fixed, so setting it to 4% makes rows at or above 4% terminal
  growth unvaluable. 21 dash cells render correctly rather than raising.

  Performance

  65 ms per slider move with both grids — 127 valuations. Not perceptible; no
  reason to cache.

  One deliberate behavior worth knowing: the sensitivity grids do not show the
  warning card when the center scenario is invalid. Each cell is its own
  scenario, so the grids stay useful and just dash out the unreachable ones. The
  other three panels still warn. My first test asserted the opposite; the code
  was right.

  Uncommitted

  dcf.py, viz.py, app.py, test_dcf.py, test_app_wiring.py. README.md needs the
  fifth tab documented — flagged, not silently rewritten. As ever, the render 
  itself is unverified, though this tab inherits the existing heatmap's colour
  and markup wholesale, so the risk is lower than for the two SVG charts.

