  The gap

  The five tabs all answer one question — what is NVIDIA worth given my 
  assumptions? — at increasing resolution. Neither of the two questions a
  research analyst gets paid to answer is covered:

  1. Where should I spend my research time?
  2. Where am I different from the market, and by how much?

  Each of these has a standard visualization, and both can be built from the
  model as it stands — no new data, no new inputs.

  ---

  1. Tornado chart — which assumption actually decides this

  Horizontal bars, one per driver, each spanning the value swing across a
  plausible range, sorted longest-first, centered on the base case. Computed
  from the live model:

  driver                    low     high    swing   crosses $180?
  Year 1 growth              85      200      114   YES
  WACC                      194       89      104   YES
  Year 5 growth              97      183       85   YES
  Year 5 op margin          111      157       46   -
  Terminal growth           116      161       45   -
  Forecast years            118      160       42   -
  Tax rate                  142      117       25   -
  Net capex                 137      123       13   -
  Working capital           136      130        5   -
  Year 1 op margin          132      135        3   -

  Only three drivers can reach $180 on their own. The other seven cannot justify
  the market price no matter where you put them in a defensible range. That
  single fact would reshape how an analyst allocates effort — and nothing
  currently in the app says it.

  It also corrects something the app currently implies. Measured per percentage
  point, WACC dominates everything (−13.9%/pt against Year-1 growth's +1.7%/pt).
  But Year-1 growth has the larger practical swing, because its plausible range
  is 50 points wide while WACC's is 5. Sensitivity per unit and sensitivity
  that matters rank differently, and only a tornado shows the second.

  Fits the existing system cleanly: diverging bars in the blue/red pair already
  in viz.py, with the same "blue is worth more" meaning. It's the cheapest of
  the two to build.

  One caveat, and it's the important one: the ranking is entirely a function of
  the ranges I picked. Those are my judgment, not research. The honest version
  makes the low/high bounds analyst-editable, or at minimum states them on the
  chart — otherwise it's a confident-looking ranking resting on invisible
  assumptions.

  ---

  2. Reverse DCF — what the market's price already assumes

  The model runs one direction: assumptions → value. An analyst needs the
  inverse, because "the market is pricing in X, and I think X is wrong" is where
  differentiated calls come from. Solving for what $180 alone requires:

    WACC                   10.0% ->    8.3%
    Terminal growth         3.0% ->    5.2%
    Year 5 op margin       55.0% ->   75.1%
    Year 1 growth          50.0% ->   68.3%
    Year 5 growth          15.0% ->   24.5%
    Net capex               1.5% -> unreachable

  The visualization I'd build is the implied revenue path: your forecast against
  the one the market's price requires, as two lines on the existing projection
  chart.

  ▎ implied year-10 revenue $1,080B vs your forecast $757B (+43%)

  That reframes the whole exercise. "$133.70 versus $180" is an argument about a
  discount rate nobody can settle. "The market needs NVIDIA at $1.08 trillion 
  of revenue in year 10, and I have it at $757B" is a claim an analyst can
  research, defend, or abandon — and it's checkable against TAM, share, and
  capacity.

  Two rows are worth flagging on sight: terminal growth of 5.2% is above
  long-run nominal GDP forever, and a Year-5 margin of 75.1% exceeds NVIDIA's
  current gross margin. Displayed as implausible-in-red, those immediately tell
  the analyst the market's optimism has to live in growth or the discount rate —
  it cannot live in margins.

  This is the more valuable of the two and the more work: it needs a bisection
  solver in dcf.py (straightforward — I wrote a throwaway one earlier this
  session) plus handling for targets that are unreachable at any input value.

  ---

  What I considered and set aside

  - Monte Carlo distribution — attractive, but in a model where terminal value
    is 60% of the answer it mostly re-expresses the tornado with added false
    precision, and it needs distribution inputs the analyst would have to
    invent.
  - Football field (DCF range against comps and 52-week range) — the standard
    analyst one-pager, but it needs market data the model doesn't have. Worth
    doing only if you want to pull in a price feed.
  - Scenario attribution waterfall (why value moved from case A to case B) —
    genuinely useful, but overlaps the waterfall tab enough to be the third
    pick, not the second.

  ---

  If you want both, I'd build the tornado first — it's cheaper, it reuses the
  existing palette and bar machinery, and its output tells you whether the
  reverse DCF is even worth building for your use case. Say the word and I'll
  plan it.

