❯ Describe to me how you would implement the following requirement: "Add a      
  bear/base/bull scenario panel with probability weighting. For each scenario,  
  the user should be able to set the key assumptions; the app shows the         
  corresponding intrinsic value and the probability-weighted average."          

● Good requirement — there's a genuine mathematical trap in it. Let me compute
  the real numbers before describing the design.

  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)

● Before the mechanics, the decision that shapes everything — and it's where
  this requirement is easy to get wrong.

  The weighting must apply to values, not assumptions

  With plausible bear/base/bull sets at 25/50/25:

  Bear  p=25%  $ 39.30   -78.2%
  Base  p=50%  $133.70   -25.7%
  Bull  p=25%  $447.06  +148.4%

  A) weight the VALUES   : $188.44   +4.7%
  B) weight the INPUTS   : $128.58  -28.6%
     difference          : $+59.86  (+46.6%)

  A 47% gap, and it flips the signal from SELL to BUY. The DCF is non-linear in
  its inputs — 1/(WACC − g) is convex — so averaging the assumptions and valuing
  once is not the expected value. Σ pᵢ·V(assumptionsᵢ) is; V(Σ pᵢ·assumptionsᵢ)
  isn't. The requirement as written ("shows the corresponding intrinsic value
  and the probability-weighted average") specifies the right one. I'd pin that
  with a test asserting the two differ, so a future simplification can't quietly
  collapse three run_dcf calls into one.

  Which assumptions are scenario-scoped

  21 inputs × 3 scenarios is 63 controls — unusable. From the tornado I ran
  earlier, only three drivers can move value across $180 alone, so I'd scope
  scenarios to five: Year-1 growth, Year-5 growth, Year-5 operating margin,
  WACC, terminal growth. Fifteen sliders.

  Everything else — tax, net capex, working capital, horizon, the per-year
  margin path — inherits from the global left-hand panel. That keeps one source
  of truth for the plumbing and means a scenario is a statement about the five
  things that matter, not a second copy of the model.

  Model layer — dcf.py

  @dataclass
  class ScenarioResult:
      name: str
      raw_probability: float
      weight: float              # normalised
      result: DCFResult | None   # None when the scenario can't be valued
      error: str | None

  @dataclass
  class WeightedValuation:
      scenarios: list[ScenarioResult]
      weighted_value: float
      valued_mass: float         # probability actually covered
      probability_above_price: float

  def weighted_valuation(base_assumptions: dict,
                         scenarios: list[tuple[str, float, dict]]) ->
  WeightedValuation:

  Each scenario's overrides land on the base assumptions through the existing 
  _apply_axis / apply_year_5_margin helpers — not a plain dict.update(). That
  matters: the Y5 margin override hits exactly the trap I fixed on the last tab,
  where an explicit operating_margins list silently beats the year_5_margin
  keyword. A naive merge would make the margin column of every scenario inert in
  per-year mode, and it would look completely fine doing it.

  UI and wiring — the one architectural departure

  Every tab so far is output-only; all inputs live in the left column. This tab
  can't follow that — 15 more sliders would swamp the panel — so the scenario 
  inputs live inside the tab, as three gr.Group columns side by side.

  That forces a second event registration:

  ┌───────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┬────────────────────┐
  │        Handler        │          Triggers          │      Outputs       │
  ├───────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ existing valuate      │ 21 global sliders          │ the 5 existing     │
  │                       │                            │ panels             │
  ├───────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┼────────────────────┤
  │ new                   │ 21 global + 18 scenario    │ the scenario panel │
  │ evaluate_scenarios    │ controls                   │                    │
  └───────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┴────────────────────┘

  Two reasons not to fold it into valuate: dragging a bear-case slider would
  otherwise recompute both 63-cell heatmaps (127 wasted valuations), and valuate
  would grow to 39 positional parameters. The scenario handler still listens to
  the global sliders, because the inherited assumptions feed it.

  I'd extend test_app_wiring.py's label-order guard to cover the second handler
  too — it's the same positional-binding hazard, and that guard has already
  caught this class of bug twice.

  Probabilities

  Three sliders, 0–100. Rather than erroring when they don't sum to 100,
  normalise and show the normalisation: entering 30/50/30 displays normalised: 
  27% / 45% / 27%. Never blocks, never silently lies. A note appears only when
  the raw sum isn't 100.

  When a scenario is individually unvaluable — WACC below terminal growth is
  reachable per-scenario — its card shows the reason, it drops out of the
  average, and the panel states weighted over 2 of 3 scenarios (75% of 
  probability mass). Silently redistributing would misreport the expectation.

  The visualization

  Four KPI cards (bear, base, bull, weighted) over a horizontal value strip:
  three labelled dots on a $/share axis, the weighted mean as a distinct marker,
  and the $180 market price as a vertical reference line. Dots take the
  existing diverging pair by which side of price they land on — same meaning as
  everywhere else in the app. Probability rides as a text label, not dot area,
  since area is a weak channel for a number that must be read exactly.

  Plus P(value > price) as a cheap, genuinely useful readout — here 25%.

  One thing I'd put on the panel itself

  That $188.44 deserves a warning label. The bull case contributes $112 of it — 
  59% of the headline from a scenario with 25% probability. A three-point
  expectation is dominated by whichever tail you draw most aggressively, so the
  number is more a statement about your bull case than about NVIDIA. I'd surface
  each scenario's contribution to the weighted value in the table, so that
  dependence is visible rather than buried.

  Effort

  Roughly the size of the waterfall tab: dcf.py gains ~60 lines and two
  dataclasses, app.py ~80 for the three slider groups and the second handler,
  viz.py ~70 for the strip, and 10–12 tests. The riskiest part isn't the maths —
  it's the second event handler, since that's new ground for this codebase's
  wiring.

  Want me to plan it properly?

