  Yes — and Gradio 6 supports it better than I'd assumed

  I should correct something first: I've avoided JavaScript all session on the
  stated grounds that "Gradio may not execute script injected through gr.HTML."
  I never verified that. Checking now, gr.HTML in Gradio 6 is effectively a
  custom-component primitive:

  ┌────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │       Parameter        │               What it gives you                │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ js_on_load             │ JS that runs on load, with element,            │
  │                        │ trigger('change'), and mutable props           │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ server_functions       │ JS calls Python directly — server.my_func(a,   │
  │                        │ b)                                             │
  ├────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ html_template /        │ Scoped templates interpolating ${value} and    │
  │ css_template           │ props                                          │
  └────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  And gr.HTML has change/input events plus a preprocess, so it can act as a
  genuine input carrying a value back to Python. The pure-CSS crosshair and
  tooltips still work fine, but they were a more conservative choice than
  necessary.

  There is no native range slider, so either route needs building.

  Option A — two cut-point sliders, no JS

  The insight that makes this cheap: three probabilities have two degrees of 
  freedom, so two handles is the correct number. Model them as cut points on a
  0–100 axis rather than as probabilities:

   0        A=25              B=75          100
   |─ Bear ─|───── Base ──────|─── Bull ────|
       25%         50%             25%

   bear = A
   base = B − A
   bull = 100 − B

  It sums to 100 by construction — no normalisation, no validation error, no
  "your probabilities add to 110%" state to design around. That alone removes a
  chunk of the complexity from the design I described earlier.

  Above the two sliders sits a read-only gr.HTML segmented bar showing the three
  proportions, coloured and labelled — so you get the visual the question is
  really after, with the sliders doing the input. Needs one small guard: a
  change handler clamping A ≤ B.

  Fully testable, zero new API surface, and it reuses the slider styling already
  consistent across the app.

  Option B — a genuinely draggable segmented bar

  Real, and cleaner than I'd have guessed:

  gr.HTML(
      value="25,75",
      html_template="<div class='seg'>…three segments, two handles…</div>",
      css_template="…scoped, uses the existing diverging palette…",
      js_on_load="""
        // pointerdown/move/up on each handle; clamp so left <= right
        props.value = `${left},${right}`;
        trigger('change');
      """,
  )

  The change event then feeds the existing handler with "25,75", parsed in
  Python. The segments are the data display and the drag target at once — no
  hidden-input hack needed.

  Which I'd recommend, and why it's not the shiny one

  Option A, and the reason is specific to this session rather than to Gradio.

  Every visual in this app is unverified — nobody has looked at five tabs. A
  pure-CSS or slider-driven control fails visibly and statically: my numeric
  tests catch wrong values, and if layout is off you'll see it immediately. A
  drag handler fails invisibly to every test I can write. pointermove logic,
  handle clamping, touch behaviour, and whether trigger('change') actually
  round-trips are all things only a browser can confirm, and I can't open one.

  So Option B is a reasonable thing to build once someone has the app open and 
  can iterate — it's maybe 40 lines of JS. Building it blind, in a session where
  the existing charts haven't been eyeballed either, is how you end up with a
  control that looks right in the markup and doesn't move under the pointer.

  Option A gets you two handles, guaranteed-valid probabilities, and the
  segmented bar visual, with nothing that can silently break.

  Either way it slots into the scenario panel design unchanged — it just
  replaces three probability sliders and deletes the normalisation logic.

  If you'd like, I can plan the scenario panel with Option A, and leave a note
  in the code marking the exact seam where Option B would drop in later.

