     monotone steps, not three categorical hues — and deliberately a neutral 
     grey ramp
     rather than blue, because blue already means "worth more" everywhere else
     in this app and
     must not start meaning "more probable". Validated against the 2:1 ordinal
     floor on both
     surfaces:

     ┌───────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────┐
     │       │   lightest   │     mid      │    darkest    │
     ├───────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
     │ light │ #aaa89d 2.39 │ #898781 3.59 │ #52514e 7.94  │
     ├───────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
     │ dark  │ #52514e 2.41 │ #898781 5.33 │ #c3c2b7 10.69 │
     └───────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────┘

     (#c3c2b7 was the obvious lightest step and fails at 1.79 on white; #aaa89d
     replaces it.)

     The probability→weights conversion lives in one function, so the truly
     draggable bar
     (js_on_load on gr.HTML) can replace the sliders later without touching
     anything else.

     Model layer — dcf.py

     @dataclass
     class ScenarioResult:
         name: str
         weight: float                 # 0-1, sums to 1 across valuable 
     scenarios
         result: DCFResult | None      # None when this case cannot be valued
         error: str | None

     @dataclass
     class WeightedValuation:
         scenarios: list[ScenarioResult]
         weighted_value: float
         valued_mass: float            # probability actually covered
         probability_above_price: float

     def probability_split(cut_a: float, cut_b: float) -> tuple[float, float,
     float]
     def weighted_valuation(base_assumptions: dict,
                            cases: list[tuple[str, float, dict]]) ->
     WeightedValuation

     Each case's overrides land on the base assumptions through the existing
     apply_year_5_margin helper (dcf.py), not a plain dict.update(). This is not
     optional: in per-year mode an explicit operating_margins list beats the
     year_5_margin
     keyword, so a naive merge would make every scenario's margin override inert
     — the exact
     bug fixed on the last tab, and equally invisible.

     UI and wiring — app.py

     The one architectural departure: every tab so far is output-only, with all
     inputs in the
     left column. Twelve more sliders there would swamp it, so the scenario 
     inputs live
     inside the tab — three gr.Columns (Bear sliders / Base read-only / Bull
     sliders) with
     abbreviated labels, since the column heading carries the context.

     That needs a second event registration:

     ┌──────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────┐
     │       Handler        │          Triggers           │      Outputs      │
     ├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────┤
     │ existing valuate     │ the 21 global sliders       │ the 5 existing    │
     │                      │                             │ panels            │
     ├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼───────────────────┤
     │ new                  │ the 21 global + 12 scenario │ the scenario      │
     │ evaluate_scenarios   │  controls                   │ panel             │
     └──────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴───────────────────┘

     Folding this into valuate would mean dragging a bear-case slider recomputes
     both 63-cell
     heatmaps — 127 wasted valuations — and pushes valuate to 33 positional
     parameters. The
     scenario handler still listens to the global sliders because Base and the
     inherited
     assumptions come from them.

     test_app_wiring.py's label-order guard gets extended to the second handler.
     Same
     positional-binding hazard; that guard has already caught this class of bug
     twice.

     Visualisation — viz.py

     The data's job is delta to a reference (each case against the $180 market
     price),
     which is the diverging-bar case — not a new form, and it reuses the
     validated pair.

     - One horizontal bar per scenario, growing left or right from a vertical 
       price line,
       in the existing blue (above price) / red (below price).
     - A fourth bar for the weighted average in the waterfall's total grey
       (#898781),
       marking it as derived rather than an input — the same distinction the
       waterfall already
       draws.
     - Each bar direct-labelled with its value, upside and probability.
     - P(value > price) as a headline readout alongside the weighted value.

     One honest affordance on the panel: at the default spread the Bull case
     contributes
     $112 of the $188 weighted value — 59% of the headline from a 
     25%-probability case. A
     three-point expectation is dominated by whichever tail is drawn most
     aggressively, so the
     table shows each scenario's contribution to the weighted value, making that
     dependence
     visible rather than buried.

     Edge cases

     - A single case can be unvaluable (its WACC at or below its terminal
       growth). That
       card shows the reason, drops out of the average, and the panel states
       weighted over 2 of 3 cases (75% of probability mass). Silently
       redistributing would misreport the
       expectation.
     - All three unvaluable → the warning card, consistent with the other
       panels.
     - Cut points at the extremes (A=0, B=100) must yield a zero-probability
       case that renders
       without dividing by anything.

     Tests

     - weighted_value == Σ pᵢ·Vᵢ exactly.
     - Weighting values ≠ weighting inputs, pinned with the 47% gap above.
     - probability_split always sums to 1; A>B is clamped; extremes give
       zero-weight cases.
     - The Y5 margin override bites in per-year mode — the apply_year_5_margin
       trap.
     - An unvaluable case is excluded and valued_mass reports the shortfall.
     - Bar geometry inverts back to the model values, as for the other two
       charts.
     - Wiring: the second handler's input order matches its signature by label.

     Verification

     1. .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q — full suite green (64 existing +
        new).
     2. Restart; confirm HTTP 200, six tabs, and two dependencies in /config —
        the
        existing one with 5 outputs and the new one with 1.
     3. Drive the live server with gradio_client: the three scenario values
        reconcile against
        direct run_dcf() calls; the weighted average equals the hand-computed
        sum; moving a
        bear slider changes only the scenario panel; a bear case with WACC below
        its terminal
        growth produces the partial-mass message.
     4. Confirm the five existing tabs are untouched, and that moving a global
        slider still
        updates both the old panels and the scenario panel.
     5. Time both handlers separately — the scenario handler runs 3 valuations,
        so it should be
        near-instant next to the grids' 65 ms.

     As with every visual here, the render is unverified — no browser tools.
     Option A was
     chosen precisely because it fails visibly and statically rather than
     silently, but nobody
     has looked at it.

     Out of scope

     - No change to the valuation math, the five existing tabs, or the global
       sliders.
     - No drag interaction (Option B) — the probability→weights seam is isolated
       so it can be
       added later against a browser.
     - No commit or push unless you ask.
     - README.md will need the new tab documented; I will flag it rather than
       rewrite it
       silently.

