  Four KPI cards (weighted value, upside + signal, P(worth more than price)),
  the segmented probability bar, diverging bars from the $180 price line, and a
  by-scenario table with each case's contribution to the weighted value.

  The two cut-point sliders work as intended

  cut 25/75 -> Bear 25%  Base 50%  Bull 25%
  cut 10/40 -> Bear 10%  Base 30%  Bull 60%   weighted $188.44 -> $312.28

  Probabilities sum to 100 by construction — there is no normalisation code, no
  validation state, and no way to enter an invalid split. Dragging one cut point
  past the other pushes it along. probability_split also clamps independently,
  so the model is correct even if the UI push ever fails.

  Correctness guards

  The non-linearity is pinned. A test asserts the value-weighted and
  input-weighted answers differ by more than 30%, so collapsing three run_dcf
  calls into one averaged valuation fails loudly rather than silently changing
  the answer by 47% and flipping the signal.

  The per-year margin trap is handled. Scenario overrides route through
  apply_year_5_margin, not dict.update() — the same bug as the sensitivity axis,
  and equally invisible. Tested in per-year mode: bear < base < bull, all three
  distinct.

  A dead case degrades honestly. Setting the bear case's WACC below its terminal
  growth gives:

  ▎ Weighted over 2 of 3 cases (75% of probability mass).

  It drops out, the remaining weights renormalise, and the shortfall is stated
  rather than quietly redistributed.

  The second handler is genuinely independent — /config shows valuate: 21 → 5
  and evaluate_scenarios: 33 → 1, and moving scenario sliders leaves the other
  five panels untouched. Both handlers have label-order guards now.

  One honest affordance on the panel

  The panel says out loud what the number hides: the Bull case supplies $112 of 
  the $188 — 59% of the weighted value from 25% of the probability. A
  three-point expectation is mostly a statement about whichever tail you drew
  hardest, so the table shows per-scenario contribution rather than burying it.

  Correction to my own estimate

  I predicted the scenario handler would be near-instant next to the grids.
  Measured over HTTP it was slower (82 ms vs 71 ms), which looked backwards.
  In-process:

  valuate (127 valuations + 2 grids):  2.7 ms
  evaluate_scenarios (3 valuations) :  0.1 ms

  The HTTP numbers were round-trip overhead, not computation — the compute ratio
  is ~27×, as expected. Worth knowing for any future performance claim in this
  app: at these sizes the network dominates, so wall-clock round trips tell you
  nothing about the model.

  Uncommitted

  dcf.py, viz.py, app.py, test_dcf.py, test_app_wiring.py. README.md needs the
  new tab documented — flagged, not rewritten.

  The probability→weights conversion is isolated in probability_split, so the
  draggable bar (Option B) can replace the two sliders later without touching
  the model, the panel, or the tests.

