     /plan to edit
     Honest buy/hold/sell recommendation in the Scenarios panel

     Context

     The Scenarios panel already prints a signal, and it is currently
     misleading. It derives
     BUY/HOLD/SELL from the probability-weighted value by the same ±15% rule the
     Valuation tab
     uses. At defaults that produces:

     weighted $188 (+4.7%)  ->  HOLD

     But the scenario range is $39 to $447, only 25% of the probability sits
     above the
     price, and the Bull case alone supplies 59% of that weighted average on 25%
     probability.
     A crisp "HOLD" on a number carried by one fat tail is exactly the false
     precision the
     requirement warns against.

     Outcome: a recommendation block that leads with the range and the 
     probability mass,
     states its own conviction, flags when its inputs disagree, and answers
     Damodaran's question
     directly — what would NVIDIA have to deliver to justify $180?

     What the feature looks like

     At the top of the Scenarios tab, above the existing cards:

     ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │ RECOMMENDATION                                                   │
     │                                                                  │
     │   SELL · low conviction                                          │
     │   Value range $40 – $450 against a $180 market price             │
     │   75% of your probability sits below the price                   │
     │                                                                  │
     │ ⚠ Your weighted average ($188) reads HOLD, but it is carried by  │
     │   the Bull case — 59% of the average from 25% of the probability. │
     │   The mass and the mean disagree; treat the mean with caution.    │
     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     WHAT $180 REQUIRES  (each driver alone, everything else at your base)

       Year 5 operating margin   55.0% ○————————● 76.5%   ✗ impossible
             operating margin cannot exceed NVIDIA's 75% gross margin
       Terminal growth            3.0% ○———● 5.2%         ✗ implausible
             above long-run nominal GDP growth, sustained forever
       Year 5 revenue growth     15.0% ○—————● 24.5%      ⚠ demanding
             implies $1,080B year-10 revenue — more than the entire
             2025 global semiconductor industry ($792B)
       Year 1 revenue growth     50.0% ○————● 68.3%       ✓ defensible
       WACC                      10.0% ●——○ 8.3%          ✓ defensible

       Two of the five routes to $180 are effectively closed. The defensible
       ones are a faster near-term ramp or a lower discount rate — so that is
       where the research effort belongs.

     Hollow dot = your assumption, filled dot = what the price requires. Each
     row is scaled to
     its own plausible range; the drivers are in different units, so there is
     deliberately
     no shared axis.

     The recommendation logic

     Two independent readings, reported together, because their disagreement is
     the signal:

     ┌─────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────┐
     │   Reading   │                    Basis                    │ At defaults │
     ├─────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
     │ Mean        │ weighted value vs. ±15% (the existing       │ HOLD        │
     │ verdict     │ signal_for)                                 │ (+4.7%)     │
     ├─────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────┤
     │ Mass        │ P(value > price) vs. 35% / 65%              │ SELL (25%)  │
     │ verdict     │                                             │             │
     └─────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────┘

     - They agree → that verdict. Conviction High if the whole range sits one
       side of the
       price, Moderate otherwise.
     - They disagree → take the more cautious of the two, force conviction to
       Low, and
       say so explicitly, naming the scenario responsible for the gap.

     This is the part that makes the output honest rather than decorative: a
     single blended score
     would hide the conflict, and the conflict is the most informative thing on
     the panel.

     Headline figures round to the nearest $10 ($40 – $450, not $39.30 – 
     $447.06). The exact
     values stay in the existing by-scenario table.

     The break-even framework

     Reverse-solve each driver for the value that makes the base case worth
     exactly the market
     price, via bisection. Computed:

     ┌─────────────────────────┬───────┬──────────┬─────────┐
     │         Driver          │ Yours │ Required │  Move   │
     ├─────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ Year 1 revenue growth   │ 50.0% │ 68.3%    │ +18.3pt │
     ├─────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ Year 5 revenue growth   │ 15.0% │ 24.5%    │ +9.5pt  │
     ├─────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ Year 5 operating margin │ 55.0% │ 76.5%    │ +21.5pt │
     ├─────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ WACC                    │ 10.0% │ 8.3%     │ −1.7pt  │
     ├─────────────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ Terminal growth         │ 3.0%  │ 5.2%     │ +2.2pt  │
     └─────────────────────────┴───────┴──────────┴─────────┘

     Plausibility needs substantive thresholds, not range membership — all five
     required values
     sit inside their own slider ranges, so "is it in range" is a useless test.
     Each verdict carries
     its basis on screen so the judgment is auditable rather than oracular:

     ┌──────────────────┬─────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┐
     │       Test       │   Verdict   │                  Why                  │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ required         │ ✗           │ an operating margin cannot exceed the │
     │ operating margin │ impossible  │  gross margin; NVIDIA's FY2025 gross  │
     │  > 75%           │             │ margin is 75%                         │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ required         │ ✗           │ above long-run nominal GDP, in        │
     │ terminal growth  │ implausible │ perpetuity                            │
     │ > 4%             │             │                                       │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ implied year-10  │ ⚠ demanding │ exceeds the entire 2025 global        │
     │ revenue > $792B  │             │ semiconductor industry                │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ required WACC <  │ ⚠ demanding │ too low for this customer             │
     │ 7%               │             │ concentration                         │
     ├──────────────────┼─────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ otherwise        │ ✓           │ within the range of reasonable        │
     │                  │ defensible  │ disagreement                          │
     └──────────────────┴─────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┘

     Thresholds live in one documented constant block in dcf.py, not scattered
     through the
     renderer, so they can be argued with and changed in one place.

     Changes

     dcf.py

     - solve_for_target(assumptions, param, lo, hi, target) — bisection,
       returning None when
       the target is unreachable in the bracket. This has been written ad hoc
       three times in this
       session's analysis; it becomes a real function. Routes year_5_margin
       through the existing
       apply_year_5_margin helper, or the solve is inert in per-year mode.
     - break_even(assumptions, target_price) → list[BreakEven] with
       (driver, label, base, required, verdict, reason, range_lo, range_hi).
     - recommend(wv) → Recommendation(verdict, conviction, mean_verdict, 
       mass_verdict, disagreement, range_lo, range_hi). Pure logic over the
       existing WeightedValuation; no new valuation.
     - The plausibility thresholds as named module constants.

     viz.py

     - render_recommendation(rec) — the headline block, reusing the .kpi card
       shape and the
       existing signal colours for the verdict.
     - render_break_even(rows) — one dumbbell per driver. Per the form heuristic
       this is the
       before→after case, so one hue: #2a78d6 light / #3987e5 dark (4.42 / 5.26
       contrast),
       hollow dot for yours, filled for required, connector in the recessive
       grey. The skill's
       "1 hue, 2 shades" is adapted because the light shade (#86b6ef) only
       reaches 2.11:1 on
       white; hollow-vs-filled carries the distinction and rings are already in
       this app's mark
       vocabulary.
     - Verdicts use the reserved status palette (#0ca30c good / #fab219 warning
       / #d03b3b
       critical) and always ship icon + label, never colour alone — light-mode
       warning is
       1.83:1 by design and the pairing is the documented mitigation.

     app.py

     No new tab and no new handler. build_scenarios gains the two blocks at the
     top of the panel
     it already returns, so the existing evaluate_scenarios wiring is untouched.

     Tests

     - solve_for_target recovers a known input: solving WACC for the base case's
       own value returns
       10%; an out-of-bracket target returns None.
     - The solver bites in per-year mode — the apply_year_5_margin trap, for the
       third time.
     - Each break-even value, fed back into run_dcf, reproduces the market price
       to the cent.
       This is the test that matters: it proves the reverse solve and the
       forward model agree.
     - Verdict thresholds: mass ≥65% → BUY, ≤35% → SELL, between → HOLD;
       conviction High only when
       the range does not straddle the price.
     - Disagreement handling: at defaults the mean says HOLD and the mass says
       SELL, so the
       output must be SELL at Low conviction and carry the explanation.
     - Each plausibility verdict fires at its threshold boundary.
     - Headline range rounds to the nearest $10.

     Verification

