  └────────┴──────────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘

  Each case tab reproduces the app's own idiom one level down — a scale=2 input
  column of all 21 controls beside a scale=3 column of the five views. All four
  panels come from one assumption_panel() helper, so they cannot drift apart; a
  test asserts all four expose identical labels in identical order.

  Live config confirms the shape: 9 top-level tabs + 15 nested, four valuate
  registrations (21→5 each), evaluate_scenarios 65→2, toggle_scenario_mode
  43→54.

  Verified live

  OFF mode   10 table rows, $133.70, 3 polylines, 5 waterfall bars, 63/63 grid
  cells
  CASE panels  Base $133.70   Bear $38.48   Bull $449.49   each from its own 21
  inputs
  SCENARIOS    weighted $188.85 · SELL / low conviction · 5 break-even drivers

  Independence is real, not cosmetic. Setting the bear case to a 7-year horizon
  moves the weighted result, puts bear's $37.60 in the panel, and leaves the
  base panel byte-identical. Cases can now differ on horizon, tax, capex and
  per-year paths — not just the five drivers.

  The bug this avoided

  The plan flagged that merging complete case dicts through _apply_axis would be
  order-dependent. Measured, it's worse than predicted — three different 
  answers:

  year_5_margin applied first   $45.01
  operating_margins first       $38.99
  run_dcf(**case) directly      $38.16

  Applying year_5_margin at all materializes a margin list, which isn't what the
  taper path means; a later operating_margins: None then wipes it.
  weighted_valuation_from_cases calls run_dcf directly, and a test pins all
  three figures so nobody re-introduces the merge.

  A free 3× speedup

  Cutting the break-even solver from 200 bisection iterations to 60 took
  evaluate_scenarios from 26.0 ms to 8.2 ms — and every break-even value still
  round-trips to exactly $180.00, which is asserted. 60 halvings is already past
  float precision; the old 200 was spending ~1,400 wasted valuations per
  driver.

  Caveats

  - The toggle's value copy is one-directional by construction: both base panels
    are written from whichever one is becoming authoritative, so it cannot
    loop. Tested both directions.
  - Four switch_detail registrations now exist — one per panel — so each case's
    taper/per-year toggle drives only its own groups.
  - README.md needs the mode documented; flagged, not rewritten.
  - Still no browser. This restructured the layout, so tab visibility, the
    nested row inside a tab, and whether 21 controls in a nested scale=2 column
    still read well are all unverified. That's a bigger unknown than usual, on
    top of six visualizations nobody has looked at.

  Nothing is committed — dcf.py, app.py, test_dcf.py, test_app_wiring.py are
  modified.

