❯ /plan                                                                         
  ⎿  Current Plan

     Scenario Analysis mode: three full cases, each with its own inputs and 
     views

     Context

     Scenarios currently live in one tab, reading five override drivers off a
     shared base. That
     makes them a sidecar rather than a way of working: you cannot give the bear
     case its own
     horizon, tax rate or per-year path, and you cannot see the bear case's own
     forecast table,
     projection chart, waterfall or sensitivity grids.

     Outcome: a Scenario analysis toggle that restructures the app into three
     complete,
     independent cases — each with a full assumption panel and the full set of
     visualisations —
     with the weighted result and recommendation promoted to the first tab.

     What the feature looks like

     Toggle off — exactly today's app, unchanged:

     ┌── left pane ──┬── tabs ────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │ ☐ Scenario    │ Valuation │ Projection │ Waterfall │ Sens ×2   │
     │   analysis    │                                               │
     │ Growth        │   (the selected view of the one model)         │
     │ Margin        │                                               │
     │ Cash flow     │                                               │
     │ Discount rate │                                               │
     └───────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Toggle on — the left pane becomes the probability split, and each case gets
     a tab:

     ┌── left pane ──┬── tabs ────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │ ☑ Scenario    │ ▸Scenarios◂ │ Base │ Bear │ Bull               │
     │   analysis    │                                               │
     │ Probability   │  RECOMMENDATION                                │
     │   split       │    SELL · low conviction                       │
     │ Bear/Base ──● │    Value range $40 – $450 vs $180              │
     │ Base/Bull ──● │  ⚠ mean and mass disagree…                     │
     │               │  [probability bar] [scenario bars] [table]     │
     │ [Bear|Base|   │  WHAT $180 REQUIRES  (break-even dumbbells)    │
     │  Bull bar]    │                                               │
     └───────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Inside any case tab — the app's own idiom, one level down:

     ┌── Bear ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │ ┌─ inputs ──┬─ views ───────────────────────────────────────┐ │
     │ │ Growth    │ Valuation │ Projection │ Waterfall │ Sens ×2  │ │
     │ │ Margin    │                                              │ │
     │ │ Cash flow │   bear case's forecast table, charts, grids   │ │
     │ │ Discount  │                                              │ │
     │ └───────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
     └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Scenarios is declared first and is selected when the mode turns on.

     The structural constraint this is built around

     Gradio cannot move a component between containers. Every component is
     declared once and
     shown or hidden. So:

     - The Scenarios tab is declared first and hidden when the mode is off —
       "first among
       visible tabs" comes for free, no reordering.
     - Tabs carry visible= and id=; gr.Tabs(selected="scenarios") returned from
       the toggle's
       event makes it active. Both verified present in this Gradio version.
     - Two sets of base inputs exist: the left-pane set (authoritative when off)
       and the
       Base-tab set (authoritative when on). The toggle copies values across
       once, one
       direction — no continuous sync, so no feedback loop. 84 sliders declared,
       ~23 visible.

     Step-by-step implementation

     1. dcf.py — value complete cases, not overrides

     weighted_valuation(base, cases) merges each case's overrides through
     _apply_axis. With
     fully independent cases that is actively wrong, and silently so:

     A complete case dict always carries both operating_margins (None in taper
     mode) and
     year_5_margin. Routing it through _apply_axis makes the result depend on
     dict order —
     apply year_5_margin first and it materialises a margin list, then applying
     operating_margins: None wipes it out. The bug would produce a plausible
     number.

     Add weighted_valuation_from_cases(cases) that calls
     run_dcf(**case_assumptions)
     directly, bypassing the merge entirely. Order-independent by construction.
     The existing
     override-based function stays for the break-even solver, which genuinely
     does perturb one
     key at a time.

     Also drop solve_for_target's iteration count from 200 to 60. Bisection on a
     float
     converges long before 200; each iteration costs two run_dcf calls, so the
     break-even block
     is doing ~2,000 valuations where ~600 would do. This is why
     evaluate_scenarios measures
     26 ms today — by far the slowest handler.

     2. app.py — declare both layouts

     Left pane: the mode gr.Checkbox, then two gr.Groups — the existing 21
     sliders
     (visible=True) and the probability split plus its bar (visible=False).

     Tab order, declared once:

     ┌─────┬────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────┐
     │  #  │                Tab                 │ Visible when │ Visible when  │
     │     │                                    │      off     │      on       │
     ├─────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
     │ 1   │ Scenarios                          │ —            │ ✓ (selected)  │
     ├─────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
     │ 2–6 │ Valuation, Projection, Waterfall,  │ ✓            │ —             │
     │     │ Sensitivity ×2                     │              │               │
     ├─────┼────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────┤
     │ 7–9 │ Base, Bear, Bull                   │ —            │ ✓             │
     └─────┴────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────┘

     Each case tab is a gr.Row of a scale=2 input column (21 sliders, the same
     section
     headings and ranges as the left pane) and a scale=3 column holding a nested
     gr.Tabs with
     the five views. Factor the slider block into a helper returning a dict of
     components so the
     four panels are built from one definition rather than copy-paste.

     3. app.py — handlers

     ┌─────────────────────────┬──────────────────┬────────────────────────────┐
     │         Handler         │      Inputs      │          Outputs           │
     ├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
     │ valuate (existing,      │ 21 left-pane     │ 5 off-mode panels          │
     │ unchanged)              │                  │                            │
     ├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
     │ valuate_case ×3         │ that case's 21   │ that case's 5 panels       │
     │ registrations           │                  │                            │
     ├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
     │ evaluate_scenarios      │ 63 case + 2 cuts │ Scenarios panel            │
     │ (reworked)              │                  │                            │
     ├─────────────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
     │ switch_mode (new)       │ toggle + 21 left │ visibilities, tab          │
     │                         │  + 21 base       │ selection, copied values   │
     └─────────────────────────┴──────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘

     valuate_case is the existing build_valuation function registered three 
     times with
     different inputs and outputs — no new valuation logic. The toggle handler
     returns
     gr.update() (a no-op) for whichever input set is not being written, so the
     copy only ever
     flows one way.

     4. viz.py

     No changes. Every renderer already takes a DCFResult or a grid.

     Tests

     - The ordering trap: a complete case dict with operating_margins=None plus
       year_5_margin set must produce the taper; with a list set, must produce
       the list. Asserted
       against weighted_valuation_from_cases, and asserted that routing the same
       dict through
       the old override path would differ.
     - solve_for_target at 60 iterations still round-trips every break-even
       value to the cent —
       the precision claim, re-verified after the cut.
     - Three wiring guards, one per case handler: component labels match the
       signature
       positionally, as for the existing two handlers. This hazard has already
       bitten twice.
     - Toggle handler: returns the right visibility pattern for each state,
       selects scenarios
       when on and valuation when off, and copies values in one direction only.
     - Independence: giving the bear case a different horizon changes the bear
       panels and the
       weighted value, and leaves the base and bull panels untouched.
     - The scenario weighting still equals Σ pᵢ·Vᵢ, and still differs from
       weighting inputs.

     Verification

     1. .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q — green (92 existing + new).
     2. Restart; confirm HTTP 200 and, in /config: 9 tab items, the Scenarios
        tab declared
        first, and the expected handler set with their input/output counts.
     3. Drive the live server: with the mode off every existing tab reconciles
        exactly as it does
        today (the off path must be byte-identical); with it on, each case panel
        reconciles against
        a direct run_dcf on that case's assumptions, and the Scenarios panel's
        weighted value
        matches Σ pᵢ·Vᵢ computed independently.
     4. Time a case-slider change: expect ~3 ms for the case handler plus the
        scenario handler's
        break-even cost, and report the figure after the iteration cut.
     5. Confirm the toggle's value copy carries the base assumptions across in
        both directions.

     Unverifiable here, and larger than usual: this restructures the layout, and
     nobody has
     looked at any of the six existing visualisations. Tab visibility, the
     nested row inside a tab,
     and whether ~23 visible controls in a scale=2 column still read well are
     all eye problems.
     I will verify the config and the numbers, and say plainly what is untested.

     Out of scope

     - No change to the valuation math or to any renderer.
     - The off-mode path must behave identically to today; it is a regression
       surface, not a
       feature surface.
     - No commit or push unless you ask.
     - README.md needs the mode documented; I will flag it rather than rewrite
