  What was actually wrong

  I traced it properly rather than guessing. Gradio's Python side was correct —
  I drove its own postprocessing and the payload was exactly right:

  toggle(True)   tabitem {'visible': False, '__type__': 'update'}
                 tabs    {'selected': 'second', '__type__': 'update'}

  So the fault was the browser not honouring a visibility update on an
  individual tab item — it kept rendering the hidden tabs in the tab bar. That's
  frontend behaviour I can't debug without a browser, so I stopped depending on
  it.

  One asymmetry in Gradio's own source supports this: Tabs.__init__ plumbs
  visible through BlockContext, while Tab.__init__ only sets it as a bare
  attribute. Hiding a container is a plain element toggle; hiding a tab item
  requires re-rendering the tab bar.

  The fix

  Two sibling gr.Tabs containers instead of one container with tabs hidden
  individually:

  [Tabs visible=True]              <- the single-model views
    Valuation · Projection · Waterfall · Sensitivity ×2

  [Tabs visible=False]             <- scenario mode
    Scenarios · Base · Bear · Bull   (each case holding its own nested views)

  tab items individually hidden:  none

  The toggle now flips 2 containers instead of 9 tab items, and the handler
  shrank from 54 outputs to 46.

  Three things fall out of this for free:

  - No selected juggling. Scenarios is literally the first tab of its own
    container, so it leads and opens active by structure.
  - The blank-pane bug class is gone. No hidden tab can be the selected one,
    which was the root cause of that earlier failure. The selected="valuation"
    workaround is no longer needed.
  - A regression guard: test_no_tab_relies_on_individual_visibility_any_more
    asserts no tab item carries visible=False, so nobody reintroduces the
    pattern the browser ignores.

  106 tests pass.

  Worth saying plainly

  This is the second browser-only failure in a row, and both were invisible to a
  suite that checks handler return values. My verification proved the payload
  was right while the screen was wrong — twice. The numeric checks are genuinely
  strong; the rendering has no coverage at all, and five visualisations remain
  unlooked-at. Please tell me if the toggle still misbehaves.

