  What I measured

  Compute was never the problem — it's 11 ms. The cost is what ships on every
  event:

  one case handler -> 5 panels
     valuation      3,568 bytes
     projection    11,398 bytes
     waterfall      3,487 bytes
     sens wacc     17,136 bytes
     sens margin   18,394 bytes
  scenario handler -> 2 panels      7,967 bytes
                                   ─────────────
  PER SLIDER MOVE                 61,950 bytes

  62 KB of HTML across seven components, re-rendering 126 heatmap cells and two
  SVGs — on every .change, which a slider fires continuously while the handle
  moves. Gradio already coalesces to one-in-flight-plus-one-pending, so the
  queue wasn't growing without bound; each round trip was just slow enough that
  a drag produced a stream of them. That's the crawl and the apparent hang.

  Two changes

  1. Recompute when a control settles, not on every step. Sliders now trigger on
  .release — once per gesture instead of dozens of times per drag. Radios and
  checkboxes keep .change; they have no release event and are cheap. A settles()
  helper picks the right event per component type, so this can't drift.

  2. No spinner for 11 ms of work. show_progress defaulted to "full", covering
  the output area each time — that animation is the "queuing" you saw. All the
  valuation handlers are now "hidden".

  Verified in the served config — the heavy handlers fire on release and show no
  progress:

  valuate              hidden   ['change', 'load', 'release']
  valuate_1/2/3        hidden   ['change', 'load', 'release']
  evaluate_scenarios   hidden   ['change', 'load', 'release']
  toggle_scenario_mode hidden   ['change']

  (change still appears because the radio in each panel is part of the same
  handler — that's intended.) 106 tests pass.

  Two things to be straight about

  There's a trade-off I made for you. Values no longer update while you drag —
  they update the instant you let go. Given 62 KB and 126 cells per frame,
  live-during-drag was never going to feel smooth, so this should read as faster
  rather than slower. If you'd rather have live updates, the real fix is
  shrinking the payload — the two sensitivity grids are 35 KB of the 54 KB, so
  moving them to their own lazier handler would let the fast panels stay live.

  Possible gap I can't test: if .release doesn't fire when you adjust a slider
  with arrow keys, keyboard adjustment would stop updating. I couldn't verify
  that without a browser. Worth a quick check — click a slider handle and press
  an arrow key.

