● Done — reload http://127.0.0.1:7860. 116 tests pass.

  What was causing the nesting

  Two containers were stacking up:

  - gr.Group paints --background-fill-secondary — that's the lighter grey outer
    box
  - Gradio then wraps the checkbox in its own bordered form inside that

  So you had a box inside a box. I swapped the Group for a plain gr.Column,
  which draws nothing, put the single border on it in CSS, and flattened the
  inner form box:

  .mode-box { border: 1px solid var(--border-color-primary); border-radius:
  10px;
              background: transparent; padding: 12px 14px 6px; }
  .mode-box .form, .mode-box .block, .mode-box .gr-box {
              border: none !important; background: transparent !important; … }

  The tree now shows one container holding both:

  column#3 ['mode-box']          <- the only box
    form#12 → checkbox#4         <- flattened, no box of its own
    column#5                     <- probability_panel
      markdown#6 ['mode-note']
      html#7                     <- segmented bar
      row#8                      <- the two cut sliders

  One judgement call I made

  You said remove the outer grey container, which left a choice between no
  container and a non-grey one. I kept a thin border with a transparent
  background, because removing the box entirely would leave the checkbox and the
  sliders looking like unrelated page elements again — the opposite of "same
  container". If you want it flatter still, deleting the border line from
  .mode-box gets you there.

  The test now matches the container by its mode-box class rather than by layout
  type, and additionally asserts the type is column and that the inner form is
  flattened — so reintroducing a Group would fail rather than silently restoring
  the grey.

