     │                                                              │
     │ Probability split                                            │
     │ ┌──────────┬───────────────────────┬──────────┐              │
     │ │ Bear 25% │       Base 50%        │ Bull 25% │  (full width)│
     │ └──────────┴───────────────────────┴──────────┘              │
     │  Bear / Base boundary ──●──    Base / Bull boundary ────●──   │
     ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Scenarios │ Base │ Bear │ Bull          ← full width now      │
     │                                                              │
     │   (the selected case, its own inputs and views)               │
     └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     The two cut sliders go side by side in a gr.Row now that they have the
     width for it —
     stacking them full-page would leave the same dead space the change is
     removing.

     Step-by-step implementation — app.py only

     1. Lift the toggle out of the Row

     scenario_mode is declared before with gr.Row():, at page level. It governs
     the whole
     layout, so reading as a page-level control is also more honest than sitting
     in one column.

     2. Lift the probability split out of the Row

     The probability_panel column moves to page level directly beneath the
     toggle, keeping
     visible=False. Inside it the heading and note stay as they are,
     probability_bar spans
     the full width, and cut_a / cut_b sit in a gr.Row.

     3. Hide the assumptions column, not just its contents

     This is the actual fix. The inner global_panel wrapper disappears and
     assumption_panel() goes straight into the outer column, which is named
     assumptions_column and is what the toggle hides:

     with gr.Row():
         with gr.Column(scale=2, visible=True) as assumptions_column:
             all_inputs, global_mode, global_groups = assumption_panel()
         with gr.Column(scale=3):
             ...the two Tabs containers, unchanged

     With that column display:none, the scale=3 sibling is the Row's only
     laid-out child and
     takes the full width.

     4. The toggle handler

     Output count and order are unchanged — 4 containers plus 42 values — only
     the first slot
     changes identity, from global_panel to assumptions_column:

     ┌──────┬─────────────────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
     │ Slot │      Component      │ Mode off │ Mode on │
     ├──────┼─────────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ 0    │ assumptions_column  │ visible  │ hidden  │
     ├──────┼─────────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ 1    │ probability_panel   │ hidden   │ visible │
     ├──────┼─────────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ 2    │ single_model_tabs   │ visible  │ hidden  │
     ├──────┼─────────────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
     │ 3    │ scenario_model_tabs │ hidden   │ visible │
     └──────┴─────────────────────┴──────────┴─────────┘

     Nothing else changes: the value copy still works because all_inputs still
     exists, merely
     hidden, and viz.py and dcf.py are untouched.

     Tab labels stay as they are

     You chose to keep the full Sensitivity names, so they are unchanged.
     Stating the consequence
     plainly rather than leaving it as a surprise: with the mode off the
     assumptions column
     still holds two fifths, so those five labels (~1,070px of tab bar in
     ~840px) will probably
     still wrap to a second row. Scenario mode is where this change buys width.
     Shortening them
     remains a one-line change if you later decide the wrap bothers you.

     Tests — test_app_wiring.py

     - The toggle hides the whole assumptions column, not an inner wrapper — the
       void-space
       fix, asserted directly.
     - A structural guard: scenario_mode and probability_panel are siblings of
       the main
       Row, not descendants of it, and that Row has exactly two children. This
       is what stops the
       controls drifting back inside a column and quietly restoring the dead
       space.
     - The existing toggle tests keep working unchanged, since the output shape
       is identical;
       their comments get the renamed component.

     Verification

     1. .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q — green (106 existing + new).
     2. Restart; confirm HTTP 200.
     3. Inspect /config's layout tree: the toggle and the probability panel sit
        above the Row,
        the Row has two children, and the assumptions column carries scale=2.
     4. Call toggle_scenario_mode both ways and confirm the four-container
        pattern, with the
        assumptions column hidden when on.
     5. Confirm the five existing panels still reconcile against the model, in
        both modes — this
        is a layout change and must not move a single number.

     The part I cannot verify: whether the scale=3 column actually expands to
     fill the row
     once its sibling is hidden. That is flexbox behaviour in Gradio's own CSS,
     and it is the
     whole point of the change. It should work — a display:none child leaves the
     flex flow — but
     after two browser-only failures in this app I am not going to claim it
     does. Please check
     that the tabs genuinely widen when you toggle.

     Out of scope

     - No change to tab labels, the valuation math, any renderer, or dcf.py /
       viz.py.
     - Mode-off behaviour must be identical apart from the toggle's new
       position.
     - No commit or push unless you ask; README.md still needs the scenario mode
       documented.

