     └──────────────────────┴───────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

     Outcome: two tiers of validation — a small set of conditions that refuse to
     value, and a
     set that values but says plainly why the answer is suspect.

     What gets implemented

     Tier 1 — blocking, raises ValueError

     Routed through the machinery that already exists: every panel shows the red
     card, and in
     scenario mode the case drops out with its probability mass reported.

     ┌──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │            Check             │                   Why                    │
     ├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ wacc <= terminal_growth      │ the perpetuity is infinite or negative   │
     │ (existing)                   │                                          │
     ├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ terminal multiple > 100×     │ (1+g)/(WACC−g) past this is arithmetic,  │
     │ (new)                        │ not valuation                            │
     ├──────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────┤
     │ shares <= 0 (existing)       │ no per-share figure exists               │
     └──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘

     Guarding the multiple rather than the raw spread is the point: a 1pp spread
     is harmless
     at a 20% WACC and ruinous at 4%, so a spread rule blocks sane inputs and
     passes mad ones.
     The message names the multiple, which is the number an analyst recognises.

     Tier 2 — warnings, the model still values

     ┌─────────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┐
     │      Check      │      Threshold       │          Message says          │
     ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Terminal        │ > 40×                │ most of the value rests on one │
     │ multiple high   │                      │  perpetuity multiple           │
     ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
     │                 │                      │ the terminal value is a        │
     │ Final-year FCF  │ < 0                  │ negative perpetuity, so the    │
     │ negative        │                      │ per-share figure has no        │
     │                 │                      │ economic meaning               │
     ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Terminal growth │ >                    │ sustained above nominal GDP,   │
     │  above long-run │ MAX_TERMINAL_GROWTH  │ in perpetuity                  │
     │  GDP            │ (4%)                 │                                │
     ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
     │ Operating       │ > NVDA_GROSS_MARGIN  │ an operating margin cannot     │
     │ margin above    │ (75%)                │ exceed the gross margin        │
     │ gross margin    │                      │                                │
     ├─────────────────┼──────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
     │ WACC below a    │ < MIN_CREDIBLE_WACC  │ hard to defend given customer  │
     │ credible floor  │ (7%)                 │ concentration                  │
     └─────────────────┴──────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────┘

     Those last three constants already exist in dcf.py, used by the break-even
     judging.
     Reusing them keeps one definition of "implausible" rather than two that can
     drift.

     Verified: the defaults trip nothing. Left pane and Base 14.7×, Bear 10.2×,
     Bull 20.8×;
     margins 62–65% against 75%; WACC 9–12% against 7%; Bull's terminal growth
     sits exactly at
     4% and the test is strictly greater. A validation layer that fires on the
     opening screen
     trains people to ignore it.

     Step-by-step implementation

     1. dcf.py — carry warnings on the result

     @dataclass
     class ModelWarning:
         code: str        # "terminal_multiple", "negative_terminal_fcf", ...
         message: str

     DCFResult gains warnings: list[ModelWarning], defaulting to empty. A
     _validate()
     helper runs after the rows are built — it needs the final-year FCF and the
     multiple — and
     returns the list. Blocking checks stay where they are, at the top of
     run_dcf, except the
     multiple check which also needs nothing but wacc/terminal_growth and so
     joins them.

     Codes, not just prose, so tests assert on behaviour rather than wording.

     2. dcf.py — move the break-even WACC bracket

     BREAK_EVEN_DRIVERS brackets WACC from 4.01%, which at a 3% terminal growth
     is a 102×
     multiple — the new block would reject it, solve_for_target would see a nan
     at the
     bracket end and report the whole row as unreachable. The bracket moves to
     5% (51.5×,
     inside the block). Without this the break-even table silently loses its
     WACC row.

     3. viz.py — render the notice

     render_warnings(warnings) returns an amber notice listing each message, or
     empty string
     when there are none. Reuses the amber treatment already built for the
     recommendation's
     mean-versus-mass disagreement (.rec-warn), so warnings look like the one
     warning the app
     already has rather than introducing a second visual language.

     4. app.py — show it

     - build_valuation puts the notice directly above the KPI cards, so it is
       the first thing
       read on the Valuation tab. This covers all four panels at once, since the
       left pane and
       the three case tabs share that function.
     - The scenario table gains a marker on any case carrying warnings, so a
       flagged bear case
       is visible without opening its tab.
     - One new CSS block for the notice; .warn (red, blocking) stays as it is.

     Tests

     - Each threshold fires at its boundary: 100× blocks and 99× does not; 40×
       warns and
       39× does not; terminal growth 4.0% is clean and 4.1% warns.
     - The defaults produce no warnings at all — for the left pane and all three
       cases. This
       is the test that stops the feature becoming noise.
     - Negative final-year FCF warns and still returns a value, per your call,
       so the case
       stays in the weighted average.
     - Warning codes survive into the rendered panel.
     - The break-even WACC row is still solvable after the bracket move, and
       every break-even
       value still round-trips to the price.
     - Sensitivity grids render the newly-blocked cells as –: at WACC 4% two of
       nine rows, at
       6% one of nine, at 8% none. Previously those cells showed numbers in the
       thousands.

     Verification

     1. .venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pytest -q — green (114 existing + new).
     2. Restart; confirm HTTP 200 and that the opening screen shows no notice.
     3. Drive the live server: WACC 4% / terminal 3.9% now returns the red card
        instead of
        $10,060; thin margins with heavy capex return a value plus an amber
        notice; the margin
        sensitivity grid at WACC 4% shows dashes where it showed four-figure
        values.
     4. Confirm the five panels and the scenario weighting are otherwise
        unchanged — this adds
        a layer, it must not move a valid number.

     As ever the rendering is unverified: the amber notice is new markup and
     nobody has seen it.
     Its colour treatment is inherited from the existing recommendation warning,
     which lowers
     the risk but does not remove it.

     Out of scope

     - No change to slider ranges. Narrowing them would prevent some of this,
       but the ranges are
       deliberately wide so analysts can stress the model; the answer is to
       flag, not forbid.
     - No change to the valuation arithmetic itself, or to any chart.
     - No commit or push unless you ask; README.md still needs the scenario mode
       documented.

