     Two corrections worth calling out

     Year-1 operating margin is 65.6%, not FY2026's 60.4%. FY2026's margin is
     depressed by a
     gross-margin dip to 71.1% against 75.0% the year before. Q1 FY2027 actual
     is back to 74.9%
     gross / 65.6% operating, and Q2 guidance (GM 74.9% ±50bp, opex ~$8.5B on
     $91.0B) implies
     65.6% again. Using the FY2026 full-year figure would carry a one-off into
     all ten
     forecast years. So DEFAULT_YEAR_1_MARGIN 0.624 → 0.656.

     Working capital is calibrated, not read off a label. The README flags this
     as the one
     default "not tied to a filing". Ex 5 reports "working capital absorption
     19.3%", but that is
     computed on a different basis than this model's % of incremental revenue.
     Solving instead
     for the value that reproduces FY2026 actual free cash flow:

     NOPAT 130,387 x (1 - 15.1%)      = 110,699
     less net capex (6,042 - 2,843)   =   3,199
     => implied working capital draw   =  10,925  on revenue growth of 85,441  =
     12.8%

     At 12.8% the model reproduces FY2026 FCF as $96.60B against an actual 
     $96.58B. That
     retires the placeholder warning. The same reconciliation independently
     confirms net capex
     1.5% (3,199 / 215,938 = 1.48%), which was already the default.

