  What was actually wrong

  Gradio ships this rule:

  .gradio-container-6-26-0 .prose * { color: var(--body-text-color) }

  That's a (0,2,0) selector setting color directly on every descendant of a
  gr.HTML. My rule was .hm-cell { color: var(--ink-l) } at (0,1,0) — so it lost
  twice over: lower specificity, and because Gradio paints the <span>s directly,
  inheritance from the cell never got a look in.

  So the <span>s carrying the value and upside took the theme's body colour
  instead of the ink computed for their own cell. At the ramp's neutral step the
  cell is #f0efec — near-white. In a dark theme that's white text on a white
  cell.

  The ink was never decorative: it's computed per cell from that cell's own
  background lightness, precisely so the text stays legible as the colour moves.
  Handing it to the theme defeated the whole mechanism.

  Fix: triple the class — .hm-cell.hm-cell.hm-cell — which wins at (0,3,0)
  without !important and without depending on Gradio's version-stamped container
  class. All four light/dark variants tripled equally, so the cascade keeps its
  original order.

  Two more places had the same bug

  Auditing every rule that inks text sitting on a background I choose turned up:

  - .sp-seg-l — the probability bar's segment labels. The Base segment is light
    grey, so a dark theme would have put white text on a near-white segment.
    Same failure, just not noticed yet.
  - .rec-verdict — the red/amber/green you specifically asked for was being
    replaced by the body colour.

  The guard caught my own fix

  I first wrote this with doubled classes. The new specificity test failed it
  immediately: (0,2,0) only ties with .prose *, and ties fall back to source
  order — which depends on where Gradio injects custom CSS. Tripling wins
  outright.

  Three tests added (135 total): ink specificity must beat .prose * in every
  variant; the specificity helper is pinned against known answers; and every
  band of both ramps must separate from its ink by a real OKLab margin, so a
  future palette edit can't quietly recreate this.

  ---

  This is the fourth bug you've found by looking that my suite couldn't. The
  pattern is consistent and worth saying plainly: I can verify numbers, not 
  appearance. The charts and layouts still haven't been seen by anyone — if the
  Projection lines, the Waterfall, or the scenario tiles look wrong too, tell me
  and I'll fix those the same way.

