● Deployed. Reload the Space to see it.

  What changed

  Gradio's default marks the active tab with accent-coloured text and a 2px 
  underline — subtle decoration next to a panel full of numbers, and with nine
  tabs across two levels in scenario mode it's easy to lose your place.

  The strip is now a recessed trough holding one raised pill. That's the same
  language the rest of the app already uses for cards — 1px border, 10px radius,
  secondary fill — so it reads as part of the app rather than a bolted-on
  control.

  The active tab differs by four things at once: lifted background, border,
  heavier weight, and full-strength ink against subdued neighbours. Deliberately
  not hue alone, so it survives greyscale and colour-vision deficiency.

  Nested tabs stay subordinate. The five views inside each Bear/Base/Bull case
  keep the same shape but smaller, with no trough of their own. Two equally loud
  strips stacked would flatten the hierarchy.

  Reading the bundle first paid off

  Gradio's component classes carry a svelte-11gaq1 hash that changes every
  release, so I keyed everything to ARIA roles instead. That turned out to
  matter more than expected — Gradio renders a second, invisible .tab-container
  as a measuring clone for its overflow menu, and its stepper component also
  uses role="tablist". Pairing .tab-container with role="tablist" hits the real
  strip and neither decoy.

  I also applied the specificity lesson from the heatmap up front rather than
  after you reported it.

  A correction worth flagging

  The specificity helper my CSS guards are built on was wrong twice: it counted
  pseudo-elements at class level, and treated legacy single-colon :after as a
  pseudo-class. That's the spelling Gradio's compiled CSS actually uses — so the
  helper was misjudging the very rules it exists to compare against. Fixed and
  pinned.

  140 tests, five new guards: rules outrank Gradio's; no svelte hash anywhere in
  the stylesheet; every .tab-container selector paired with its role; active
  tab differs by more than colour; nested strip stays subordinate.

  ---

  Same caveat as always: I can't see it. The tests prove the rules will win the 
  cascade, not that the result looks good. Concrete dials if it's off:

  - Pill too loud or too quiet → the box-shadow and font-weight: 680 on
    .selected
  - Tabs too tall → padding: 7px 14px
  - Want the accent colour back on the active label → one line, I removed it
    because Gradio's orange accent sits oddly against this app's blue/red/grey
    palette

